1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,584
Viral game ideas with Laex05. 

2
00:00:01,584 --> 00:00:03,570
Hi, everybody! I’m Laex05.

3
00:00:03,570 --> 00:00:05,238
If you haven't met me before,

4
00:00:05,238 --> 00:00:06,573
I'm the creative director at Vidyuu LLC.

5
00:00:06,573 --> 00:00:10,377
Technically, I own the company,
but, like, I just I work on vision.

6
00:00:10,493 --> 00:00:13,013
You know,
I help sculpt some worlds sometimes.

7
00:00:13,013 --> 00:00:14,581
Sometimes I come in at the end of a build.

8
00:00:14,581 --> 00:00:16,099
Sometimes I'm there at the beginning.

9
00:00:16,099 --> 00:00:17,967
Sometimes I'm there throughout.

10
00:00:17,967 --> 00:00:21,287
But point is,
I’ve built quite a few worlds over the years,

11
00:00:21,538 --> 00:00:24,441
and I'm excited to share with you
everything I know.

12
00:00:24,441 --> 00:00:27,360
And so, without further ado,
we'll move on to the next slide.

13
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:29,429
Our agenda for today—
we're going to cover some basics

14
00:00:29,429 --> 00:00:32,132
of what makes things
kind of exciting and fun,

15
00:00:32,132 --> 00:00:36,086
and potentially viral. We're also going
to be looking at some example worlds

16
00:00:36,086 --> 00:00:39,089
and looking at
what made those worlds pop.

17
00:00:39,272 --> 00:00:41,341
We're also going to look at Horizon
Algorithm.

18
00:00:41,341 --> 00:00:44,778
What is there today
that we need to be tracing and tracking?

19
00:00:45,745 --> 00:00:46,913
I don't know the algorithm,

20
00:00:46,913 --> 00:00:50,100
but I can share with you what I've heard
and kind of what I've seen work.

21
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:52,802
We're going to be talking
about progressive depth—

22
00:00:52,802 --> 00:00:54,921
And depth
referring to depth of knowledge.

23
00:00:54,921 --> 00:00:58,191
So how much do you as a user know
about your game? When you go viral

24
00:00:58,191 --> 00:00:59,392
they don't know as much.

25
00:00:59,392 --> 00:01:02,429
So how do you grow that depth over time,
but also make it not

26
00:01:02,429 --> 00:01:05,632
required for the new users
who are showing up en mass?

27
00:01:06,449 --> 00:01:07,951
Then I'll have some recommendations
for you.

28
00:01:07,951 --> 00:01:11,821
And then at the end we have time for Q&A.
So we'll just keep on

29
00:01:11,988 --> 00:01:14,908
jiving. 
The basics! All right.

30
00:01:14,908 --> 00:01:18,778
Making your users feel something
and allowing them to create action.

31
00:01:18,778 --> 00:01:20,180
I don't want to keep reading the slides,

32
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:21,865
so I'm going to try
to kind of go more freeform.

33
00:01:21,865 --> 00:01:23,032
But, the point is

34
00:01:23,032 --> 00:01:27,387
that when a user shows up in your world,
they want to have an impact on the world.

35
00:01:27,387 --> 00:01:29,422
And I call that creating action.

36
00:01:29,422 --> 00:01:30,774
And the easiest way to think about

37
00:01:30,774 --> 00:01:35,161
that is like effects—visual effects,
sound effects, haptic effects.

38
00:01:35,512 --> 00:01:37,097
We're all in the mindset right now,

39
00:01:37,097 --> 00:01:41,518
or we should be in the mindset, of like
mobile development. Developing for mobile.

40
00:01:41,518 --> 00:01:45,872
So haptics might not be top of mind,
but you really want to make sure

41
00:01:45,872 --> 00:01:51,444
your worlds are both amazing on mobile
and amazing in VR.

42
00:01:51,744 --> 00:01:55,098
And haptic feedback is one of those things
that's easy to forget,

43
00:01:55,115 --> 00:01:58,118
especially when you're developing
on the desktop editor

44
00:01:58,201 --> 00:02:01,137
and you're just building
and you don't go into VR as often.

45
00:02:01,137 --> 00:02:02,539
I've been developing on the desktop editor

46
00:02:02,539 --> 00:02:05,575
for nearly a year now, and yeah,
I don't go into VR nearly enough.

47
00:02:05,575 --> 00:02:08,845
So the most recent game I built,
the haptics were a little too strong.

48
00:02:08,845 --> 00:02:11,698
Even though we had them on light,
we needed to change them to very light

49
00:02:11,698 --> 00:02:12,799
and just change them up.

50
00:02:12,799 --> 00:02:15,802
But I wasn't getting into VR enough,
so fortunately I had some testers

51
00:02:15,802 --> 00:02:16,519
who were able to be like,

52
00:02:16,519 --> 00:02:18,838
“Hey, this is a problem.”
And I was like, “Oh, thank you.”

53
00:02:18,838 --> 00:02:20,840
But definitely—
got to make sure you've got haptics.

54
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:24,661
The feedback that you get from a haptic
pulse is very satisfying,

55
00:02:25,428 --> 00:02:29,883
and these satisfaction,
these sort of feelings are very primal.

56
00:02:29,883 --> 00:02:33,953
When you get like a pop-up, 
or the pop-up effect,

57
00:02:33,953 --> 00:02:37,524
like when a confetti bursts
or any sort of effect happens

58
00:02:37,524 --> 00:02:40,760
because you did something,
that releases a very primal feeling.

59
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:41,661
I see this in my daughter.

60
00:02:41,661 --> 00:02:44,731
She's two and a half years old now,
and when she accomplishes

61
00:02:44,731 --> 00:02:47,634
something, she goes around yelling,
“Oh my gosh, I did it, I did it!”

62
00:02:47,634 --> 00:02:48,418
“Can you believe it?”

63
00:02:48,418 --> 00:02:50,270
“Give me a high five, dad, I need a hug.”

64
00:02:50,270 --> 00:02:51,838
“This is the most amazing thing ever.”

65
00:02:51,838 --> 00:02:53,690
And
like I said, it's just very primal.

66
00:02:53,690 --> 00:02:58,344
When you create action,
it causes this sort of inner excitement.

67
00:02:58,645 --> 00:03:02,031
And so visual effects,
sound effects, and haptic feedback are ways

68
00:03:02,031 --> 00:03:05,034
that users can feel the action
and see the action.

69
00:03:05,068 --> 00:03:07,136
You can also provide them with pop-ups.

70
00:03:07,136 --> 00:03:08,821
I recommend being careful with pop-ups.

71
00:03:08,821 --> 00:03:11,274
I've definitely overused these
in my experience.

72
00:03:11,274 --> 00:03:13,443
I need to be a little bit more toning down

73
00:03:13,443 --> 00:03:17,180
because people will start toning them out,
but you can use pop-ups to convey

74
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:22,168
information. Like, you hit so-and-so
or you gained X amount of money,

75
00:03:22,952 --> 00:03:26,522
and using pop-ups can definitely
be very satisfying for the user

76
00:03:26,522 --> 00:03:29,342
to learn
more about what's going on in the world.

77
00:03:29,342 --> 00:03:31,761
Motion
and scale are examples of additional types

78
00:03:31,761 --> 00:03:34,764
of ways that users can create action
that isn't just effects.

79
00:03:34,764 --> 00:03:37,584
So, if something moves. If you

80
00:03:37,584 --> 00:03:40,703
punch something and it moves around,
even just like a punching bag,

81
00:03:41,671 --> 00:03:43,256
which we've all seen, right?

82
00:03:43,256 --> 00:03:45,775
But that, like, that's very satisfying.

83
00:03:45,775 --> 00:03:48,895
Scale, physics objects,
just like you name it.

84
00:03:49,262 --> 00:03:51,631
Anything that can give the user
the feeling

85
00:03:51,631 --> 00:03:54,617
that they've created
the action is very exciting.

86
00:03:54,834 --> 00:03:58,771
And so you can use these basics
to create simple,

87
00:03:58,771 --> 00:04:02,425
addictive loops that people are going
to want to continue doing over and over.

88
00:04:02,692 --> 00:04:05,245
And so I say use effects always.

89
00:04:05,245 --> 00:04:08,281
And I'm sure there's a case
where this doesn't apply,

90
00:04:08,281 --> 00:04:09,699
but really try your best

91
00:04:09,699 --> 00:04:13,853
to include like a visual effect,
a sound effect, and haptic feedback

92
00:04:14,070 --> 00:04:17,724
wherever you're building these sort of
like miniature loops and experiences.

93
00:04:18,474 --> 00:04:20,660
So we're gonna quickly
look at some viral hit worlds

94
00:04:20,660 --> 00:04:22,729
and see what they did that worked.

95
00:04:22,729 --> 00:04:24,447
And, I'm going to just like

96
00:04:24,447 --> 00:04:27,300
prepare you, like,
get a glass of ice to dump on your head

97
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:29,369
because the first world is Hit
People Simulator.

98
00:04:29,369 --> 00:04:32,121
I know, I know—
We're ripping this Band-Aid off early.

99
00:04:32,121 --> 00:04:34,490
This is actually a great world
for quite a few reasons,

100
00:04:34,490 --> 00:04:37,193
and I think it's very important
that we take a look at worlds

101
00:04:37,193 --> 00:04:40,647
that are in the top of the menu
and understand why are these great worlds.

102
00:04:40,647 --> 00:04:43,650
What makes them really successful?

103
00:04:43,766 --> 00:04:46,586
And the thing that I found after
sitting in there for half an hour

104
00:04:46,586 --> 00:04:49,589
with just three other kids
just continuously playing

105
00:04:49,706 --> 00:04:51,991
is that you have
a small number of players,

106
00:04:51,991 --> 00:04:55,345
and this social experience
allows for each of the players

107
00:04:55,495 --> 00:04:58,681
to have a say
and an impact on the experience.

108
00:04:58,998 --> 00:05:02,819
When you have 16 players or 32 players,
suddenly you feel like

109
00:05:02,819 --> 00:05:05,271
you have to sit in the corner
and not be a participant.

110
00:05:05,271 --> 00:05:09,108
But being a small player experience,
you really got to have an impact on

111
00:05:09,108 --> 00:05:12,078
what people are talking about.
The stories they were telling.

112
00:05:12,078 --> 00:05:13,196
And this is the other part.

113
00:05:13,196 --> 00:05:16,132
This is a tell your own story
type of location.

114
00:05:16,132 --> 00:05:18,534
This isn't like a prescribed story.

115
00:05:18,534 --> 00:05:20,520
The kids get to be like
they're in the toy box,

116
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:23,189
like they're at home with their friends
making believe.

117
00:05:23,189 --> 00:05:26,209
And this is what I saw
continuously in this world.

118
00:05:26,909 --> 00:05:29,245
I think that is part of the reason
it was so successful

119
00:05:29,245 --> 00:05:30,980
and it's still so successful.

120
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:32,815
And there's constantly creating action.

121
00:05:32,815 --> 00:05:33,783
Everywhere you look

122
00:05:33,783 --> 00:05:37,487
you're bopping things around,
you're interacting with things.

123
00:05:37,637 --> 00:05:41,574
You create action everywhere you look,
and you get a lot of creativity.

124
00:05:41,574 --> 00:05:44,544
So, I think that's what makes this world
so successful.

125
00:05:44,544 --> 00:05:45,928
Do I think it's the perfect world?

126
00:05:45,928 --> 00:05:49,399
No! But we've seen obviously
it's had quite a massive hit

127
00:05:49,399 --> 00:05:52,852
with the younger audiences, and I think
there's a lot we can take away from that.

128
00:05:52,852 --> 00:05:55,488
So, that was our first one to look at.

129
00:05:55,488 --> 00:05:55,938
Next up

130
00:05:55,938 --> 00:05:59,642
we're going to take a look at Murder
Village, Murder Manor, and Spin the Bottle.

131
00:05:59,642 --> 00:06:01,711
What do these three worlds have in common?

132
00:06:01,711 --> 00:06:02,545
You want to be it.

133
00:06:02,545 --> 00:06:06,933
I think this is something that you can use
to your advantage in most worlds.

134
00:06:07,367 --> 00:06:08,634
It's random selection.

135
00:06:08,634 --> 00:06:10,053
Whether we're talking about random role

136
00:06:10,053 --> 00:06:12,355
selection like in these theory
worlds, where in Murder Village

137
00:06:12,355 --> 00:06:14,006
you get to be the murderer,
in Murder Manor

138
00:06:14,006 --> 00:06:15,475
you have a couple different roles
you can be

139
00:06:15,475 --> 00:06:17,543
whether that's murderer, sheriff,
or innocent.

140
00:06:17,543 --> 00:06:20,880
And in Spin the Bottle, you get to be
the person who gets to make the decision.

141
00:06:21,481 --> 00:06:23,966
But that random selection means
you want to keep playing

142
00:06:23,966 --> 00:06:25,701
because you want to be it, right?

143
00:06:25,701 --> 00:06:26,702
So if you're in Murder Village,

144
00:06:26,702 --> 00:06:30,123
you really want to be the murderer,
and that takes a while.

145
00:06:30,123 --> 00:06:31,741
You have to get signed up on a list.

146
00:06:31,741 --> 00:06:34,010
You've got to wait a few rounds
until you're it.

147
00:06:34,010 --> 00:06:37,730
And then once you get that hit of
being it, you want to do that again.

148
00:06:37,730 --> 00:06:40,733
So you stay for another few sessions
until you're it again.

149
00:06:40,733 --> 00:06:44,187
And I really think that's something
you can implement, whether it's

150
00:06:44,187 --> 00:06:47,323
role selection, you can also use this
in the latest world I built with Ash.xo.

151
00:06:47,457 --> 00:06:51,260
We have random cars being generated

152
00:06:51,511 --> 00:06:54,447
and people
determine the cars by buying them.

153
00:06:54,447 --> 00:06:57,083
And so if you buy a brand new vehicle
and you want to see that

154
00:06:57,083 --> 00:07:00,086
vehicle, you're going to stay for longer
until that vehicle appears.

155
00:07:00,169 --> 00:07:04,307
So using this sort of random selection
to get people to hang out longer

156
00:07:04,307 --> 00:07:08,094
and wait to see the thing that they want.
And if that thing is really satisfying,

157
00:07:08,094 --> 00:07:09,979
like getting to be it,

158
00:07:09,979 --> 00:07:13,649
or getting that vehicle that maybe it
gives you an extra boost of money,

159
00:07:14,033 --> 00:07:16,803
that then keeps them there
for a little bit longer

160
00:07:16,803 --> 00:07:18,020
because they're like,
“Oh, that was amazing.”

161
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:19,889
“I want to feel that again.”

162
00:07:19,889 --> 00:07:23,126
And I think one of the things
that I would share here—

163
00:07:27,213 --> 00:07:28,131
[Laughs]
Sorry, I'm going

164
00:07:28,131 --> 00:07:31,350
to say this politely,
my experiences in Murder Village

165
00:07:31,350 --> 00:07:34,937
have been less than ideal
because being it is so rare.

166
00:07:35,087 --> 00:07:37,890
So when I went to build Murder Manor,

167
00:07:37,890 --> 00:07:40,793
I made sure that you could be the murderer
more frequently.

168
00:07:40,793 --> 00:07:43,913
So in Murder Manor,
it's a 1 in 5 chance. In Murder Village 

169
00:07:43,913 --> 00:07:46,649
it can be that way
if there's not a lot of people signed up,

170
00:07:46,649 --> 00:07:49,368
but you can also see where there's ten
people signed up,

171
00:07:49,368 --> 00:07:52,371
and you could play for two hours
and not be the murderer in Murder Village.

172
00:07:52,488 --> 00:07:57,560
But in Murder Manor, you're going to be
it 25 or 20% of the time, roughly.

173
00:07:58,427 --> 00:08:00,930
And it's pretty much guaranteed

174
00:08:00,930 --> 00:08:03,933
that you'll get to be it
at least once within an hour.

175
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:06,352
I'd like to make
that even more guaranteed

176
00:08:06,352 --> 00:08:09,472
because it is possible using random chance,
like we do in Murder Manor,

177
00:08:09,472 --> 00:08:13,459
that you could go missing it over and over
and over again for over an hour.

178
00:08:13,459 --> 00:08:15,711
And when that happens,
people feel really bad

179
00:08:15,711 --> 00:08:17,280
and it kind of ruins the experience.

180
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:18,631
So I'd love to put a counter in that

181
00:08:18,631 --> 00:08:20,666
tracks how many times are they not murderer

182
00:08:20,666 --> 00:08:22,502
and then be like, oh,
if it's the 10th time,

183
00:08:22,502 --> 00:08:24,170
we guarantee they're going to be murderer.

184
00:08:24,170 --> 00:08:29,692
So, okay. Random selection,
pretty awesome. Social experiences.

185
00:08:29,692 --> 00:08:32,879
These three also are huge
social experiences.

186
00:08:33,446 --> 00:08:38,901
The way the users control
these experiences is unbelievable. So,

187
00:08:40,319 --> 00:08:41,521
let's start with Spin the Bottle

188
00:08:41,521 --> 00:08:44,507
because it's the one that
we've heard a lot about recently.

189
00:08:44,507 --> 00:08:47,343
And in Spin the Bottle,
the users can make up their own rules.

190
00:08:47,343 --> 00:08:50,329
There isn't a lot of prescribed
rules to the game.

191
00:08:50,396 --> 00:08:52,732
And so if you want to say
like it's a truth

192
00:08:52,732 --> 00:08:55,735
or dare version of Spin
the Bottle, you have to answer a question

193
00:08:55,735 --> 00:08:57,086
otherwise you're getting shot.

194
00:08:57,086 --> 00:09:00,089
People can make their own rules up
and that is really fun.

195
00:09:00,239 --> 00:09:02,658
I see the same thing in Murder Manor
where you see sometimes

196
00:09:02,658 --> 00:09:05,661
people playing it differently.
So sometimes people will play it like,

197
00:09:05,695 --> 00:09:08,965
“Oh yeah, I'm the murderer.”
Or sometimes they'll lie and sometimes,

198
00:09:08,965 --> 00:09:12,535
you know, there's all sorts of variants
on the way that people play the game,

199
00:09:12,985 --> 00:09:15,688
creating differences
in how that experiences.

200
00:09:15,688 --> 00:09:18,541
And same thing is true in Murder Village,
depending on how people engage

201
00:09:18,541 --> 00:09:19,308
and how they play,

202
00:09:19,308 --> 00:09:22,011
they can kind of make their own
experience, which makes it a lot of fun.

203
00:09:22,011 --> 00:09:27,199
So, social can cause a crafting
of the experience that allows,

204
00:09:27,199 --> 00:09:30,202
in my opinion, the world to be a long-

205
00:09:30,202 --> 00:09:33,205
lasting hit. With a game that is more

206
00:09:34,307 --> 00:09:37,310
uniformly the
same and it's always the same experience,

207
00:09:37,410 --> 00:09:40,079
I feel like that's going to die off
relatively quickly.

208
00:09:40,079 --> 00:09:41,213
But if you can have something

209
00:09:41,213 --> 00:09:44,717
that the users who are playing
it change the experience,

210
00:09:45,685 --> 00:09:47,987
that has a lot more staying power

211
00:09:47,987 --> 00:09:50,039
because every time you play,
depending on who you're playing with,

212
00:09:50,039 --> 00:09:51,841
it can be a completely different
experience.

213
00:09:51,841 --> 00:09:55,077
Another example of this
that I want to mention about Spin

214
00:09:55,077 --> 00:09:58,197
the Bottle is I've never played
with my friends, which is really sad.

215
00:09:58,197 --> 00:09:59,849
I've only played it 4 or 5 times

216
00:09:59,849 --> 00:10:02,602
and I've only played it with kids,
and they always shoot me.

217
00:10:02,602 --> 00:10:04,020
And I never have a good experience

218
00:10:04,020 --> 00:10:07,023
because I'm the adult in the room
and they're like, “Ah, get him out of here.”

219
00:10:07,106 --> 00:10:10,042
But I've heard from my friends
who do play Spin the Bottle that if you go

220
00:10:10,042 --> 00:10:13,579
into an instance with just people
you know, it's an incredible experience.

221
00:10:13,879 --> 00:10:15,681
And at some point
I should give that a shot,

222
00:10:15,681 --> 00:10:19,352
but just another example
of how the people you're with can really

223
00:10:19,368 --> 00:10:20,453
shape the experience.

224
00:10:21,621 --> 00:10:24,056
We're going to move into Murder Manor.

225
00:10:24,056 --> 00:10:27,259
And here I wanted to talk about some of
the retention tactics that we implemented.

226
00:10:27,827 --> 00:10:28,794
I'm not saying these are perfect

227
00:10:28,794 --> 00:10:30,212
or that you need
to put all of these in your world,

228
00:10:30,212 --> 00:10:33,199
but I just thought I'd share a little bit
of what worked for us.

229
00:10:33,265 --> 00:10:36,168
And so the first thing that we got to talk
about is the Daily Streak.

230
00:10:36,168 --> 00:10:40,256
Now this is not going to hook
every single person, but we have at least

231
00:10:40,256 --> 00:10:44,460
one user with well over 300 days
straight of playing Murder Manor,

232
00:10:44,543 --> 00:10:46,729
which breaks my brain. I don't think I—

233
00:10:46,729 --> 00:10:49,799
I definitely didn't expect
that when we created this world.

234
00:10:50,549 --> 00:10:53,552
But yeah, they're very quickly
approaching a full year of playing

235
00:10:53,552 --> 00:10:55,071
and we'll probably see that continue.

236
00:10:56,789 --> 00:10:59,909
So the daily streak, for the users
who know about it

237
00:10:59,909 --> 00:11:02,928
and who care about it, encourages them
to come back every day

238
00:11:02,928 --> 00:11:04,697
to make sure they're getting that
daily streak,

239
00:11:04,697 --> 00:11:06,932
which is incredible for your retention,
obviously.

240
00:11:06,932 --> 00:11:11,037
So, implementing a daily streak
is relatively easy to implement.

241
00:11:11,237 --> 00:11:12,054
If you're in Code Blocks,

242
00:11:12,054 --> 00:11:15,057
there's a way that you can get
the number of days since the epoch.

243
00:11:15,207 --> 00:11:16,158
And in TypeScript,

244
00:11:16,158 --> 00:11:21,297
you can get the milliseconds since epoch
and divide it by 24 times 60 times

245
00:11:21,297 --> 00:11:24,300
60 times a thousand, which is the number
of milliseconds in a day.

246
00:11:25,251 --> 00:11:26,969
And that gives you the number of days
since the epoch.

247
00:11:26,969 --> 00:11:29,238
And basically you can compare that
to the last day they were there.

248
00:11:29,238 --> 00:11:32,758
And if it is minus one,
then you can set it to more.

249
00:11:32,992 --> 00:11:36,028
It's a little oversimplifying it,
but daily streaks are relatively simple

250
00:11:36,028 --> 00:11:37,646
to implement.
Would highly encourage you to do that.

251
00:11:39,749 --> 00:11:42,902
And moving on to the next bit—
leveling up.

252
00:11:42,902 --> 00:11:43,919
I find leveling up

253
00:11:43,919 --> 00:11:47,940
is something that we can say
is akin to a lot of different things.

254
00:11:48,541 --> 00:11:51,293
In my opinion,
what this means for users of Murder Manor

255
00:11:51,293 --> 00:11:55,681
specifically is that when they come back,
they retain where they left off.

256
00:11:55,948 --> 00:11:58,951
There's this sort of sense of
I didn't lose anything

257
00:11:59,118 --> 00:12:02,121
by
leaving the world and coming back.

258
00:12:02,154 --> 00:12:05,124
And so, there's a lot of ways
you can implement that just generically

259
00:12:05,124 --> 00:12:06,692
across a lot of other worlds,

260
00:12:06,692 --> 00:12:09,145
but for us
you have a level above your head.

261
00:12:09,145 --> 00:12:11,480
There really isn't like
anything in the world you get to keep,

262
00:12:11,480 --> 00:12:14,483
but when you come back,
you have this,

263
00:12:14,667 --> 00:12:17,169
I would like—
This is a terrible way to describe it,

264
00:12:17,169 --> 00:12:19,255
but it's kind of like a prestigious-ness
in the world.

265
00:12:19,255 --> 00:12:21,257
People know you are higher level.

266
00:12:21,257 --> 00:12:22,558
That's kind of exciting.

267
00:12:22,558 --> 00:12:25,578
We've also made that
this level has a specific color set to it.

268
00:12:25,578 --> 00:12:28,581
So you see users who are brand
new have a light blue,

269
00:12:28,581 --> 00:12:31,467
and then if you're relatively new,
it's a darker blue.

270
00:12:31,467 --> 00:12:35,721
And then it changes to a purple, and
then it goes to gold, then it goes to red.

271
00:12:35,905 --> 00:12:38,808
And we are about to see our first users
hit the next color tier,

272
00:12:38,808 --> 00:12:39,859
which is super exciting.

273
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:42,978
And when people do hit the next tier,

274
00:12:42,978 --> 00:12:45,631
we are like looking out for that,
and we make a big deal about it.

275
00:12:45,631 --> 00:12:47,516
We have a whole party inside the world.

276
00:12:47,516 --> 00:12:51,203
We record a video, post it to Instagram,
and build a whole social presence

277
00:12:51,203 --> 00:12:54,990
about this whole leveling up and why it's
this kind of really amazing experience.

278
00:12:55,224 --> 00:12:57,543
So, yeah, leveling up

279
00:12:57,543 --> 00:13:00,112
and you see people just like
very proud of their levels.

280
00:13:00,112 --> 00:13:01,914
It's very exciting.

281
00:13:01,914 --> 00:13:03,048
Daily reward box.

282
00:13:03,048 --> 00:13:04,800
This is another like easy one.

283
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:07,136
I think this is much better
than the daily streak.

284
00:13:07,136 --> 00:13:09,438
I think daily streak is great
for the users that it works for,

285
00:13:09,438 --> 00:13:12,241
but daily reward box is one
that's very tangible.

286
00:13:12,241 --> 00:13:16,111
You go in, you collect your reward
in the basement, and then you feel

287
00:13:16,111 --> 00:13:19,098
that immediate satisfaction
of having gotten your daily reward.

288
00:13:19,982 --> 00:13:22,968
And so,
I think this is a lot more tangible

289
00:13:23,302 --> 00:13:25,471
and I just like the way that works.

290
00:13:25,471 --> 00:13:28,491
It's very similar to the daily streak,
but it's actually giving you something.

291
00:13:28,757 --> 00:13:30,893
In Murder Manor where
we don't have anything to give users,

292
00:13:30,893 --> 00:13:34,013
what we give them is experience,
which helps them level up faster,

293
00:13:34,013 --> 00:13:36,932
right? So you get an XP
bonus every day that you log in.

294
00:13:36,932 --> 00:13:40,169
And I think a way that you can make this
even better is every day

295
00:13:40,169 --> 00:13:43,172
they log in, you increase the amount
that's in the box.

296
00:13:43,222 --> 00:13:47,576
So for us, we increase it
by ten XP per day that you come back.

297
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:50,696
So if you've been to Murder Manor
ten days, you get a bonus of an extra

298
00:13:50,696 --> 00:13:52,431
hundred experience.

299
00:13:52,431 --> 00:13:53,549
And it starts at 100.

300
00:13:53,549 --> 00:13:56,035
So it's like doubled
after ten days of playing.

301
00:13:56,035 --> 00:14:00,139
And so for our user at 300 days,
they're getting what, 300 times that ten.

302
00:14:00,139 --> 00:14:02,191
So 3,000 on top of that, it's pretty insane.

303
00:14:04,193 --> 00:14:05,327
Pretty cool.

304
00:14:05,327 --> 00:14:08,697
So we talked about the random roles
being a retention tactic.

305
00:14:08,697 --> 00:14:10,232
I don't think
I need to describe this too much,

306
00:14:10,232 --> 00:14:13,402
but what I will share is we have that 1 in 5 
chance to be murderer,

307
00:14:13,402 --> 00:14:16,405
and we have a 1 in N chance
to be the sheriff.

308
00:14:16,438 --> 00:14:19,225
Where if you are just a single player,
one over one,

309
00:14:19,225 --> 00:14:22,228
so then you are 100% chance of being the
sheriff.

310
00:14:22,344 --> 00:14:24,296
You can play Murder Manor solo.

311
00:14:24,296 --> 00:14:26,882
You can go collect the keys,
unlock the front door, and escape.

312
00:14:26,882 --> 00:14:28,968
It is a game
that you can play by yourself.

313
00:14:28,968 --> 00:14:30,986
I wouldn't say
that's the most fun experience.

314
00:14:30,986 --> 00:14:34,857
What's nice about that, though, is that
people can still join during that window.

315
00:14:35,007 --> 00:14:37,977
So if somebody arrives late,
they are guaranteed to be the murderer

316
00:14:37,977 --> 00:14:41,430
and suddenly it's sheriff versus murderer
and it's a lot of fun.

317
00:14:42,331 --> 00:14:45,351
So, you might be having
an unexpected guest

318
00:14:45,351 --> 00:14:48,387
while you're working on finding your keys.
And then if you have two players,

319
00:14:48,387 --> 00:14:51,040
you have a 50% chance of being sheriff.
If you've got three, it can

320
00:14:51,040 --> 00:14:52,308
so on and so forth.

321
00:14:52,308 --> 00:14:54,960
It does mean
the sheriff role is a lot harder to

322
00:14:56,095 --> 00:14:57,930
get because of this—

323
00:14:57,930 --> 00:14:59,298
Like if it's a 16 player world, it's

324
00:14:59,298 --> 00:15:02,301
a 1 in 16 chance
when you have all 16 people there.

325
00:15:02,334 --> 00:15:05,838
And so what we've done because being
the sheriff is so important

326
00:15:05,838 --> 00:15:09,391
and we wanted to make sure
that you could be sheriff more than like

327
00:15:09,391 --> 00:15:13,562
once per 16, is we've made it
so that if you are a skilled player,

328
00:15:13,796 --> 00:15:16,882
you can seek out the gun
when the sheriff is murdered.

329
00:15:16,882 --> 00:15:21,470
So if you have the sheriff pass away,
we send out a pop-up notification

330
00:15:21,470 --> 00:15:24,740
to all of the innocent players
that tells them what floor

331
00:15:24,740 --> 00:15:28,794
the gun has been is placed on,
and then they can all go searching out.

332
00:15:28,794 --> 00:15:30,362
And that creates its own
social engagement.

333
00:15:30,362 --> 00:15:31,730
Where it’s like
“Did you find it? Did you find it?”

334
00:15:32,998 --> 00:15:35,100
And so
like that has a pretty fun effect as well,

335
00:15:35,100 --> 00:15:38,087
but it also allows another person
to get to be the sheriff.

336
00:15:39,471 --> 00:15:42,291
So increasing those odds, which is nice.

337
00:15:42,291 --> 00:15:44,810
This next slide is about winning
more than 50% of the time.

338
00:15:44,810 --> 00:15:46,912
I don't know if we've done this
well enough, but

339
00:15:46,912 --> 00:15:48,480
like I feel like we're doing pretty good.

340
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:51,867
And so the general rule of thumb is that
players want to feel like they're winning

341
00:15:51,867 --> 00:15:55,137
more than 50% of the time, otherwise
they feel like they're losing overall.

342
00:15:55,137 --> 00:15:56,972
And that's just not a fun experience.

343
00:15:56,972 --> 00:15:59,959
And so I think we see winning a little bit
differently here.

344
00:15:59,959 --> 00:16:01,343
So you can win by escaping.

345
00:16:01,343 --> 00:16:03,512
You can win by
just having all the murderers pass away

346
00:16:03,512 --> 00:16:04,346
if you're on the innocents.

347
00:16:04,346 --> 00:16:07,166
And the reverse is true
if you're the murderer.

348
00:16:07,166 --> 00:16:09,018
And if you have a large number
of murderers,

349
00:16:09,018 --> 00:16:11,337
you're more likely
to want to win as a group.

350
00:16:11,337 --> 00:16:15,024
And this kind of creates
a good like group winning chance.

351
00:16:15,024 --> 00:16:16,825
So you can imagine—

352
00:16:16,825 --> 00:16:18,594
Anyway,
so I'm not going to go too crazy on that.

353
00:16:18,594 --> 00:16:22,314
But what I think is important here
is that there's other ways to feel

354
00:16:22,314 --> 00:16:24,350
like you're winning inside this game.

355
00:16:24,350 --> 00:16:26,001
So you might feel like you're winning

356
00:16:26,001 --> 00:16:29,838
if you get a single kill as a murderer.
You might feel like you're winning

357
00:16:29,838 --> 00:16:32,808
if you got to be the murderer.
You might feel like you're winning

358
00:16:32,808 --> 00:16:34,910
if you got a couple keys
and put them in the locks,

359
00:16:34,910 --> 00:16:36,478
or maybe you were the last person.

360
00:16:36,478 --> 00:16:38,514
I feel like
there's just a lot of different ways

361
00:16:38,514 --> 00:16:41,784
to give them that sense of winning,
even if they're not winning.

362
00:16:42,418 --> 00:16:45,320
And I feel like that helps. Anyway,

363
00:16:45,320 --> 00:16:47,906
I don't know if we're doing it perfectly,
but I like how we're doing it.

364
00:16:48,957 --> 00:16:51,477
Easy to play.
Murder Manor is relatively easy to play.

365
00:16:51,477 --> 00:16:54,530
You can come in. You walk in
and if you're an innocent,

366
00:16:54,530 --> 00:16:56,098
you're walking around. You find a key.

367
00:16:56,098 --> 00:16:57,633
You have to discover where the door is.

368
00:16:57,633 --> 00:16:59,101
You put the key in the lock.

369
00:16:59,101 --> 00:17:00,986
Pretty straightforward.

370
00:17:00,986 --> 00:17:02,087
Same thing if you're a murderer, right?

371
00:17:02,087 --> 00:17:03,772
You grab a weapon, you slash somebody.

372
00:17:03,772 --> 00:17:05,207
If you're a sheriff, you've got a gun.

373
00:17:05,207 --> 00:17:07,743
That's probably the hardest role because
you have to click your index trigger.

374
00:17:07,743 --> 00:17:09,178
And honestly, there's quite a few people

375
00:17:09,178 --> 00:17:11,630
who are still struggling
to figure that out,

376
00:17:11,630 --> 00:17:13,015
especially new VR users.

377
00:17:13,015 --> 00:17:14,700
I've definitely,
when I take my friends and family in,

378
00:17:14,700 --> 00:17:17,703
they can never figure out how to use
weapons.

379
00:17:18,070 --> 00:17:21,023
Like, “How do I grab stuff?
How do I pull index triggers?”

380
00:17:21,023 --> 00:17:23,609
It just doesn’t makes sense
for newer VR users, but

381
00:17:24,827 --> 00:17:26,161
that is that.

382
00:17:26,161 --> 00:17:28,130
One thing I want to say,
now that I'll be talking

383
00:17:28,130 --> 00:17:31,200
a little bit more in a minute is about
one of the biggest downfalls of Murder

384
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:34,286
Manor that I really want to work out
in the next version of it,

385
00:17:35,070 --> 00:17:37,556
with like next version
being like a complete rewrite.

386
00:17:37,556 --> 00:17:42,061
But the idea is that when people
join this world, I see a frequent number

387
00:17:42,377 --> 00:17:46,281
that come in
and they're waiting for the game to end,

388
00:17:46,915 --> 00:17:49,134
and the timer is like 1 and a half
minutes and like,

389
00:17:49,134 --> 00:17:51,687
“I don't want to wait 1 and a half minutes.”
And we see people bump out of here.

390
00:17:51,687 --> 00:17:53,222
And when we're talking
about mobile development,

391
00:17:53,222 --> 00:17:56,208
which we'll be talking about
very soon too, is

392
00:17:57,209 --> 00:18:00,813
people need to get into the action 
within 20 seconds. Like 10 seconds, ideally.

393
00:18:01,230 --> 00:18:04,917
And you can't have a game like this
for mobile development.

394
00:18:04,917 --> 00:18:06,902
It needs to be like
you can continue walking in.

395
00:18:06,902 --> 00:18:09,738
And so one of the ways that we're looking
at fixing that in Murder Manor

396
00:18:09,738 --> 00:18:10,322
is to make it

397
00:18:10,322 --> 00:18:14,426
so that the game is continuously open
and running, and I don't know exactly

398
00:18:14,426 --> 00:18:17,429
if that's going to work or not, but
we're going to play around with the idea.

399
00:18:17,463 --> 00:18:20,215
But you can imagine, if you walk
in, you just get assigned a role.

400
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:22,951
And the game just kind of keeps rolling.

401
00:18:22,951 --> 00:18:24,203
And maybe there's a way to kill everybody.

402
00:18:24,203 --> 00:18:27,272
I don't know, but we'll we'll be playing
with that idea in the future.

403
00:18:27,272 --> 00:18:28,974
But just—

404
00:18:28,974 --> 00:18:30,843
Designing worlds and coming up with ideas

405
00:18:30,843 --> 00:18:33,829
that are...

406
00:18:34,830 --> 00:18:35,447
already

407
00:18:35,447 --> 00:18:38,400
having that idea in mind from
the beginning will make it a lot easier.

408
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:38,634
Right?

409
00:18:38,634 --> 00:18:41,687
So we're kind of trying to fix
a world where I'm seeing people

410
00:18:41,687 --> 00:18:44,690
leaving actively because of this timer.

411
00:18:45,541 --> 00:18:47,943
One other suggestion I've had for some of

412
00:18:47,943 --> 00:18:51,330
my students
is that we want to have the last 30 seconds

413
00:18:51,713 --> 00:18:56,635
be when it closes up, so they only have to wait a 
maximum of 30 seconds or maybe even 20 seconds.

414
00:18:56,635 --> 00:19:01,440
And at that point you say, okay, games—
We're in the final scene.

415
00:19:01,740 --> 00:19:04,977
We'll pause there
and continue moving on. Growing depth.

416
00:19:05,844 --> 00:19:06,211
Okay—

417
00:19:06,211 --> 00:19:08,847
So when you start Murder Manor,
you don't need to know a lot.

418
00:19:08,847 --> 00:19:09,648
It's pretty easy to play

419
00:19:09,648 --> 00:19:13,669
without a lot of knowledge,
but you get more depth as you play.

420
00:19:13,702 --> 00:19:15,204
You learn more about the game.

421
00:19:15,204 --> 00:19:18,090
You learn about the key positions,
the weapon positions, where are things hidden.

422
00:19:18,090 --> 00:19:21,960
You learn about where the reward box is.
You learn where the power up bonus box is.

423
00:19:22,194 --> 00:19:26,298
You learn how to become the sheriff.
And all of these things make the game more

424
00:19:26,298 --> 00:19:30,452
fun the longer you play, and reward
people who do play for longer.

425
00:19:30,452 --> 00:19:34,039
So, I think it's important to have the game
very simple and easy to play—

426
00:19:34,590 --> 00:19:35,958
Any world you're building—

427
00:19:35,958 --> 00:19:37,593
But then give something to people

428
00:19:37,593 --> 00:19:40,579
who stay for longer and spend time
learning the game,

429
00:19:40,829 --> 00:19:42,798
so it's not just the same thing.

430
00:19:43,882 --> 00:19:45,567
Next up is Power Wash and Pop Pop.

431
00:19:45,567 --> 00:19:48,570
These are less violent games.
[Laughs]

432
00:19:48,687 --> 00:19:54,543
So, what are these worlds?
Progressive difficulty.

433
00:19:54,543 --> 00:19:57,529
Okay, so—
This is the question that was just asked,

434
00:19:57,529 --> 00:19:58,197
<i>“What are the worlds?”</i>

435
00:19:58,197 --> 00:20:01,617
So Power Wash is a game where you show up
and you clean gunk off of cars.

436
00:20:02,334 --> 00:20:05,721
Pop Pop is a world where you show up
and you pop balloons.

437
00:20:06,054 --> 00:20:08,840
Relatively simple game mechanics.

438
00:20:08,840 --> 00:20:12,327
The thing that makes these
similar is the fact

439
00:20:12,327 --> 00:20:14,129
that they both have progressive
difficulty.

440
00:20:14,129 --> 00:20:18,050
The longer you play Pop Pop, the more the
balloons get harder, and bigger, and larger.

441
00:20:18,417 --> 00:20:21,420
And Power Wash, the harder the gunk gets
and the harder it is to clean off.

442
00:20:22,070 --> 00:20:23,338
And you have to level up.

443
00:20:23,338 --> 00:20:26,425
So you have your power
washer gets leveled up. In Pop Pop,

444
00:20:26,425 --> 00:20:29,428
you have these
playful guns that get leveled up and

445
00:20:30,512 --> 00:20:31,463
eventually it creates this

446
00:20:31,463 --> 00:20:34,499
what I call good insanity,
where things are just kind of like crazy.

447
00:20:34,499 --> 00:20:38,387
I think one of the things that inspired
Pop Pop when I first created it is

448
00:20:38,387 --> 00:20:41,189
I was playing a ton of Bloons Tower
defense on my phone,

449
00:20:41,189 --> 00:20:43,558
and I really wanted to be able
to do that in VR.

450
00:20:43,558 --> 00:20:46,261
And if you've ever played Bloons Tower
Defense,

451
00:20:46,261 --> 00:20:49,014
the balloons that are coming in
get insane.

452
00:20:49,014 --> 00:20:50,599
It’s just there's so many, it's impossible.

453
00:20:50,599 --> 00:20:52,768
We can't recreate that experience
in Horizon,

454
00:20:52,768 --> 00:20:55,404
at least not that I'm aware of,
because there's just too much—

455
00:20:55,404 --> 00:20:58,040
And there's too many physics objects,
and it's three-dimensional,

456
00:20:58,040 --> 00:21:00,442
and that's a whole
other talk on its own.

457
00:21:00,442 --> 00:21:01,860
But the point is,

458
00:21:01,860 --> 00:21:05,697
Pop Pop tried to emulate that
by getting larger and larger balloons.

459
00:21:05,697 --> 00:21:08,717
So you had maximum
20 balloons in the scene at a time.

460
00:21:09,601 --> 00:21:12,137
But they got so massive,
it was just insane.

461
00:21:12,137 --> 00:21:15,691
And Power Wash is very similar where the cars
get harder and harder to clean,

462
00:21:15,958 --> 00:21:19,711
and they also reward you
with a lot of money the harder they are.

463
00:21:19,711 --> 00:21:20,879
So that's pretty,

464
00:21:22,281 --> 00:21:22,914
pretty fun—

465
00:21:22,914 --> 00:21:25,817
Good insanity. Satisfying effects.

466
00:21:25,817 --> 00:21:27,753
Both of these have very satisfying
effects.

467
00:21:27,753 --> 00:21:29,237
The dings are literally the same.

468
00:21:29,237 --> 00:21:32,941
I'm using the exact same sound effect
in both of these for the popping.

469
00:21:32,941 --> 00:21:33,992
It's like ding-ding.

470
00:21:33,992 --> 00:21:37,763
It's so, so satisfying.
And so using a very satisfying effect,

471
00:21:37,763 --> 00:21:41,783
and then a visual effect, and then haptic
feedback as you're using the

472
00:21:42,050 --> 00:21:46,038
weapons in each of these. And then user
upgrades, they both have user upgrades.

473
00:21:46,038 --> 00:21:48,890
So the user has a real impact
on the power that they have,

474
00:21:48,890 --> 00:21:51,593
and I find that users
having control is fun.

475
00:21:51,593 --> 00:21:54,329
What I will say, is two things.

476
00:21:54,329 --> 00:21:59,001
Number one, I feel like both of these
are more geared towards an adult audience.

477
00:21:59,001 --> 00:22:02,454
I have not seen a staying power
in either of these with younger—

478
00:22:02,821 --> 00:22:06,325
Younger audiences. And I think it's
because there's too many variables,

479
00:22:06,508 --> 00:22:10,228
and that's hard for the younger audiences
to really grasp and work with.

480
00:22:10,579 --> 00:22:14,282
And so I love both of these games,
but I think they really are more for

481
00:22:14,583 --> 00:22:16,168
kind of an adult or an

482
00:22:17,369 --> 00:22:19,287
older teen audience.

483
00:22:19,287 --> 00:22:21,923
The other thing I will mention for both of
these is that they're difficult to balance.

484
00:22:21,923 --> 00:22:25,644
These both having progressive difficulty
means that you have to balance

485
00:22:25,644 --> 00:22:29,581
the power of the
the user versus the power of the level.

486
00:22:29,848 --> 00:22:34,736
And also, when you have a new user arrive,
how does that new user play into what's

487
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:35,987
really difficult to play?

488
00:22:35,987 --> 00:22:37,489
Because it's usually—

489
00:22:37,489 --> 00:22:39,057
You know, people have been playing
for more than ten minutes

490
00:22:39,057 --> 00:22:42,027
and after ten minutes you
get to these kind of crazy levels.

491
00:22:42,027 --> 00:22:45,030
And then hours later, it's even worse. So—

492
00:22:45,113 --> 00:22:47,632
we did our best in Power Wash
to try to balance this out.

493
00:22:47,632 --> 00:22:51,269
So we have an easy bay
where it takes the lowest level player

494
00:22:51,269 --> 00:22:54,272
in the world
and uses that to spawn a car for them.

495
00:22:54,439 --> 00:22:58,377
And then the hard bay spawns the hard car
for the hardest players in the world.

496
00:22:58,710 --> 00:23:00,062
And then it's random selection,

497
00:23:00,062 --> 00:23:02,297
so the hard bay
can still have easy cars in it.

498
00:23:02,297 --> 00:23:05,784
And if the low level players are
really high, then you can still get

499
00:23:05,967 --> 00:23:07,352
hard cars in the easy bay.

500
00:23:07,352 --> 00:23:08,987
So, there's a little bit of that there.

501
00:23:08,987 --> 00:23:12,691
But the balancing act in Power Wash
took way longer than I expected.

502
00:23:12,691 --> 00:23:16,111
So we had the game built in
like a week, but then it was nearly

503
00:23:16,111 --> 00:23:20,282
three weeks of just tweaking the numbers
to get it to play right

504
00:23:20,282 --> 00:23:23,402
where the—
you didn't get overpowered too quickly.

505
00:23:23,635 --> 00:23:25,670
And with Pop Pop,
I really think we got lucky.

506
00:23:25,670 --> 00:23:29,875
I don't know how, but I built it
in two days and the math just worked well.

507
00:23:30,092 --> 00:23:31,793
But this one I spent three weeks literally

508
00:23:31,793 --> 00:23:35,931
tweaking numbers until it felt like
the game was progressing at a decent pace

509
00:23:36,932 --> 00:23:39,868
for both users new and old.

510
00:23:39,868 --> 00:23:42,621
So, I do think the balancing act here
is very hard

511
00:23:42,621 --> 00:23:45,624
and I wouldn't recommend
anybody try to tackle this,

512
00:23:45,891 --> 00:23:48,894
unless they're willing
to spend a lot of time tweaking numbers.

513
00:23:49,044 --> 00:23:52,998
So, that is Power Wash and Pop Pop. 
All right—

514
00:23:52,998 --> 00:23:55,917
Next up, we're looking at Splash
and Horizon Royale.

515
00:23:55,917 --> 00:23:58,937
So, there is so much
I can say about these two worlds alone.

516
00:23:58,937 --> 00:24:02,240
It could be a talk on its own,
but here are a few key takeaways.

517
00:24:02,958 --> 00:24:05,310
Both of these are social hangout spaces.

518
00:24:05,310 --> 00:24:06,711
And I know at the very beginning

519
00:24:06,711 --> 00:24:09,414
we talked about this like,
“Oh man, I hope it's not just games.”

520
00:24:09,414 --> 00:24:13,702
Splash is a very perfect example
of not being a game. But Horizon Royale,

521
00:24:13,735 --> 00:24:15,687
most of you wouldn't think of it

522
00:24:15,687 --> 00:24:19,591
being a social hangout space.
But for my experience, that's what it was.

523
00:24:19,591 --> 00:24:21,710
It was a social lubricant.

524
00:24:21,710 --> 00:24:23,578
The game itself was not important.

525
00:24:23,578 --> 00:24:25,347
Sure,
there was a lot of depth to the game.

526
00:24:25,347 --> 00:24:26,548
You could learn how to use the weapons.

527
00:24:26,548 --> 00:24:29,701
There was a lot of special features,
and I had so much fun building a game

528
00:24:29,701 --> 00:24:33,088
that I thought was like,
“Oh, it's this really cool royale PvP game.”

529
00:24:33,421 --> 00:24:35,941
And then at the end of the day,
it was really just people talking about

530
00:24:35,941 --> 00:24:39,211
life and having a great time.
Laughing, making jokes.

531
00:24:40,078 --> 00:24:43,131
And the reason people stayed
was so that they could hang out and talk.

532
00:24:43,131 --> 00:24:46,251
And, you know, the game itself—

533
00:24:46,251 --> 00:24:47,602
Like I said, it's a social lubricant.

534
00:24:47,602 --> 00:24:49,104
It really allowed you to

535
00:24:49,104 --> 00:24:51,389
start talking with people
you wouldn't have otherwise talked with.

536
00:24:51,389 --> 00:24:55,560
So, you can definitely use games
to create social experiences.

537
00:24:55,560 --> 00:24:58,547
And I've always felt that
Horizon is a social space,

538
00:24:58,697 --> 00:25:02,117
and so anything that leans into social
is going to have more

539
00:25:02,484 --> 00:25:05,687
staying power in my mind,
or it's going to be a lot more fun.

540
00:25:06,955 --> 00:25:07,856
I do think Royale

541
00:25:07,856 --> 00:25:10,976
is a little too complex
for the younger generations.

542
00:25:10,976 --> 00:25:12,527
And there's a lot of things
we're going to say bad

543
00:25:12,527 --> 00:25:16,231
about these two in a second, but for now
we're going to say social was amazing.

544
00:25:17,132 --> 00:25:19,551
And Splash had tons of hangout spots.

545
00:25:19,551 --> 00:25:20,785
There was lots of places—

546
00:25:20,785 --> 00:25:24,472
You have the DJ booth,
you've got like the—all the places.

547
00:25:24,472 --> 00:25:27,509
There's a little fire pit in the back
and lots of places

548
00:25:27,509 --> 00:25:31,012
you can hang out and just chat with people.
And lots of little activities.

549
00:25:31,012 --> 00:25:33,048
And each of these activities
was really small.

550
00:25:33,048 --> 00:25:35,450
They didn't have to be
these massive game loops.

551
00:25:35,450 --> 00:25:36,117
They could just be,

552
00:25:36,117 --> 00:25:39,421
“Hey, I'm going down the water slide.”
Or, “I'm going to go do the obstacle course.”

553
00:25:39,905 --> 00:25:42,474
Or, “I'm going to sit at the
fire and smoke a marshmallow.”

554
00:25:43,441 --> 00:25:44,576
That's not how you say that.

555
00:25:44,576 --> 00:25:47,245
Roast a marshmallow. Excuse me.

556
00:25:47,245 --> 00:25:50,482
And so the point is that
those little experiences

557
00:25:50,482 --> 00:25:52,167
are also social lubricant.

558
00:25:52,167 --> 00:25:53,902
And there was
things for you to discover.

559
00:25:53,902 --> 00:25:55,337
There was a little bit of depth
there, right?

560
00:25:55,337 --> 00:25:59,274
So, you might have discovered
that if you put a floatie on

561
00:25:59,274 --> 00:26:01,526
somebody else,
you got attached to that person

562
00:26:01,526 --> 00:26:03,962
and floated with them.
And that became really fun,

563
00:26:03,962 --> 00:26:05,430
and people
had to share that with each other.

564
00:26:05,430 --> 00:26:07,098
And there was just all these little,

565
00:26:07,098 --> 00:26:10,101
little things that you could discover
as you played.

566
00:26:11,303 --> 00:26:14,422
And there was, yeah—there was just lots to
discover. Lots of little things.

567
00:26:14,539 --> 00:26:16,291
So it didn't need a massive game loop.

568
00:26:16,291 --> 00:26:18,627
It just had lots of miniature loops.

569
00:26:18,627 --> 00:26:21,596
The eventual death of both of these.
I mean, Splash and Horizon Royale are

570
00:26:21,596 --> 00:26:23,064
still getting visitors to this day.

571
00:26:23,064 --> 00:26:25,100
So like saying eventual death...

572
00:26:25,100 --> 00:26:28,887
But it feels like it because Royale,
within the first week or two,

573
00:26:28,887 --> 00:26:30,288
you had 5,000 visitors.

574
00:26:30,288 --> 00:26:33,775
It's just an insane number of people,
even in today's world, for me at least.

575
00:26:34,342 --> 00:26:35,777
I think that's a ton.

576
00:26:35,777 --> 00:26:39,681
But it died very quickly after that.
And Splash was super successful

577
00:26:39,681 --> 00:26:42,751
for the first few months
and we didn't continue updating,

578
00:26:42,751 --> 00:26:44,219
which was a big part of the reason.

579
00:26:45,470 --> 00:26:48,707
But the
point is, I feel like, I could be wrong—

580
00:26:48,857 --> 00:26:51,843
But I feel like what
we generated in both of these worlds

581
00:26:51,843 --> 00:26:54,829
was something that was really unique
and really exciting.

582
00:26:55,080 --> 00:26:58,083
And once it became less
unique and less exciting, once

583
00:26:58,083 --> 00:27:02,387
people started seeing it in other worlds,
it was less of a draw.

584
00:27:02,387 --> 00:27:05,390
So, Royale launched and had—

585
00:27:08,093 --> 00:27:09,361
[...]

586
00:27:09,361 --> 00:27:14,516
It had, I think one of the first times
we saw maps being loaded randomly,

587
00:27:14,516 --> 00:27:14,816
right?

588
00:27:14,816 --> 00:27:17,969
And I remember,
I think I thought every creator in Horizon

589
00:27:17,969 --> 00:27:20,455
had come and stopped by
to see this unique thing.

590
00:27:20,455 --> 00:27:22,223
And it was still in a gray box state
at that time,

591
00:27:22,223 --> 00:27:24,209
that first week
when it was just blowing up.

592
00:27:24,209 --> 00:27:25,644
So there was nothing to look at visually,

593
00:27:25,644 --> 00:27:26,878
it was just all gray boxes.

594
00:27:26,878 --> 00:27:31,466
But what you got to see was tiles
being placed randomly on the grid,

595
00:27:31,733 --> 00:27:35,387
and then you could go and play on the grid
in this kind of random map generation.

596
00:27:35,637 --> 00:27:37,372
And what I love about this
is that we inspired

597
00:27:37,372 --> 00:27:41,159
loads of creators to go and make map
generating worlds where they—

598
00:27:41,276 --> 00:27:43,411
You saw Mighty Tiny
is probably the most prominent example,

599
00:27:43,411 --> 00:27:46,398
but you can load into that
with a different map every time you play.

600
00:27:46,398 --> 00:27:48,750
And I'm so glad we got to inspire that.

601
00:27:48,750 --> 00:27:51,052
Whether or not
they actually saw our world, I don't know.

602
00:27:51,052 --> 00:27:54,055
But the point is
that was what I had hoped to accomplish

603
00:27:54,873 --> 00:27:56,875
by making Royale. So—

604
00:27:57,842 --> 00:27:58,877
It was unique at first, but

605
00:27:58,877 --> 00:28:02,814
then immediately after that, it's now
just another Super Rumble. Another PvP

606
00:28:02,814 --> 00:28:05,817
and there's plenty of those out there. So—

607
00:28:06,468 --> 00:28:08,370
Not unique anymore.

608
00:28:08,370 --> 00:28:09,621
It also is—

609
00:28:09,621 --> 00:28:12,407
It was also flawed in the beginning
because there was tons of Horizon bugs.

610
00:28:12,407 --> 00:28:14,159
And if I had to make a recommendation

611
00:28:14,159 --> 00:28:17,312
to anybody running into a Horizon bug that's
preventing the build of their world,

612
00:28:17,312 --> 00:28:20,298
we spent four months with the Horizon team
fixing the bug.

613
00:28:20,515 --> 00:28:22,133
I would have either republished it, 

614
00:28:22,133 --> 00:28:24,803
might have been an option because at
the time that's how the algorithm worked.

615
00:28:24,803 --> 00:28:26,004
Not today. That's not a problem.

616
00:28:26,004 --> 00:28:29,007
That's why we see Horizon Royale still
getting traffic pretty much every day.

617
00:28:30,258 --> 00:28:33,261
But the...

618
00:28:34,112 --> 00:28:36,781
recommendation would be to just stop
developing that mechanic.

619
00:28:36,781 --> 00:28:38,366
Report it to Meta. Do your due diligence,

620
00:28:38,366 --> 00:28:40,318
but go find something else to develop

621
00:28:40,318 --> 00:28:42,921
because the asset spawning bug
was so broken,

622
00:28:42,921 --> 00:28:44,939
if you had multiple players
they saw different things.

623
00:28:44,939 --> 00:28:46,508
It was just like, really—

624
00:28:46,508 --> 00:28:49,461
I'm assuming it's fixed
because we spent four months fixing it.

625
00:28:49,461 --> 00:28:52,947
But if you run into a bug like that
that's like game-breaking, breaks

626
00:28:52,947 --> 00:28:53,398
your mechanic,

627
00:28:53,398 --> 00:28:56,785
it's probably best to just shelf
that idea and work on something else.

628
00:28:58,286 --> 00:29:01,289
But that’s just my two cents.

629
00:29:02,056 --> 00:29:06,511
The next thought there is Splash.

630
00:29:06,511 --> 00:29:08,630
We gave out those scripts
and I'm glad we did.

631
00:29:08,630 --> 00:29:11,099
And we got to see this start showing up
in other worlds.

632
00:29:11,099 --> 00:29:13,918
Like Kate Austin's world had a great
implementation of the water slide,

633
00:29:13,918 --> 00:29:16,921
which I really enjoyed. And...

634
00:29:17,238 --> 00:29:19,224
But you start 
seeing like the climbing mechanics

635
00:29:19,224 --> 00:29:21,509
and all these things that we had shown off
on how to build

636
00:29:21,509 --> 00:29:23,511
appearing in other worlds.
So it stops being less—

637
00:29:23,511 --> 00:29:27,081
But I do think, you know, Waffle literally
told me he's like, “You could just like,”

638
00:29:27,081 --> 00:29:30,385
“continue updating Splash
and it'll stay stay popular.”

639
00:29:30,752 --> 00:29:32,003
And I think he's right.

640
00:29:32,003 --> 00:29:33,021
And we didn't like we went

641
00:29:33,021 --> 00:29:36,207
and worked on other projects
and just kind of did other things.

642
00:29:36,841 --> 00:29:38,576
So eventually,
it's kind of fallen to this place

643
00:29:38,576 --> 00:29:40,195
where I still hear people talk about it
and they're like, “Yeah,”

644
00:29:40,195 --> 00:29:42,080
“I wish you'd continue
working on Splash.” And, eh

645
00:29:43,648 --> 00:29:46,468
I've got other projects I'm working on
now, but

646
00:29:46,468 --> 00:29:51,122
anyway. So I think that also alludes
to my recommendation for worlds

647
00:29:51,122 --> 00:29:52,290
you want to build are worlds

648
00:29:52,290 --> 00:29:56,094
that don't require you to continuously
update it to be successful.

649
00:29:56,094 --> 00:29:57,595
So something like Murder Manor,

650
00:29:57,595 --> 00:30:00,598
where I haven't really put an update in in
six months

651
00:30:00,832 --> 00:30:01,466
is nice.

652
00:30:01,466 --> 00:30:03,535
It's very nice to be able
to work on other projects.

653
00:30:03,535 --> 00:30:05,270
Like that's my brain.

654
00:30:05,270 --> 00:30:07,789
My brain likes to do multiple things.
I know not everybody's like that.

655
00:30:07,789 --> 00:30:10,792
So just kind of work with your
your brain, right?

656
00:30:11,209 --> 00:30:12,627
Player motion is uncomfortable.

657
00:30:12,627 --> 00:30:14,279
Both of these worlds had a player motion.

658
00:30:14,279 --> 00:30:15,947
That's a bad thing.

659
00:30:15,947 --> 00:30:17,999
New users cannot do player motion.

660
00:30:17,999 --> 00:30:20,718
I definitely attribute
some of the death of these

661
00:30:20,718 --> 00:30:23,121
to being the fact
that you were being moved.

662
00:30:24,289 --> 00:30:26,808
And in Splash, it was a little bit
more like you expected it,

663
00:30:26,808 --> 00:30:26,975
right?

664
00:30:26,975 --> 00:30:29,978
You're stepping on a water slide
or whatever it is.

665
00:30:30,211 --> 00:30:31,613
In Murder Manor, you're getting blown up.

666
00:30:31,613 --> 00:30:34,616
So maybe a
little less expected, but

667
00:30:34,949 --> 00:30:35,300
yeah.

668
00:30:35,300 --> 00:30:37,852
Point is: try not to use player motion.

669
00:30:37,852 --> 00:30:40,588
It can be very uncomfortable.

670
00:30:40,588 --> 00:30:41,773
And Royale was over complicated,

671
00:30:41,773 --> 00:30:42,407
We talked about that.

672
00:30:42,407 --> 00:30:44,943
So moving on to the next slide.

673
00:30:44,943 --> 00:30:45,326
All right.

674
00:30:45,326 --> 00:30:47,328
We're talking about the Horizon
algorithm now.

675
00:30:47,328 --> 00:30:50,064
And we're specifically—
we're going to address mobile game

676
00:30:50,064 --> 00:30:53,902
development kind of in depth
a little bit here

677
00:30:55,169 --> 00:30:57,772
because this is Meta's priority.

678
00:30:57,772 --> 00:31:01,259
We need to recognize that we should be
following what Meta is looking for.

679
00:31:01,259 --> 00:31:02,660
and they want us to be developing
for mobile.

680
00:31:02,660 --> 00:31:05,163
This doesn't mean
you do need to be mobile-first.

681
00:31:05,163 --> 00:31:07,999
This means you should be thinking,
“If I'm building a VR world,”

682
00:31:07,999 --> 00:31:10,518
“it should be playable on mobile as well.”
It doesn't need to be perfect.

683
00:31:10,518 --> 00:31:11,970
It doesn't need to be the best
mobile game.

684
00:31:11,970 --> 00:31:13,821
It can still take a minute to get in,

685
00:31:13,821 --> 00:31:16,824
but it should be playable on mobile
at the very least.

686
00:31:17,208 --> 00:31:21,980
Mobile-first though, means that in
less than 20 seconds you need to be to the fun.

687
00:31:22,347 --> 00:31:26,267
And this is because sometimes people
coming in on mobile only have one minute.

688
00:31:26,601 --> 00:31:30,655
Like that's real,
and I will attest that I am one of them.

689
00:31:30,655 --> 00:31:34,976
I've literally been waiting outside
of a restaurant for my food to come out,

690
00:31:35,159 --> 00:31:38,963
and I will be jumping into Horizon
playing Power Wash. And I'll have one

691
00:31:38,963 --> 00:31:40,582
or maybe two minutes to play,

692
00:31:40,582 --> 00:31:43,434
and then somebody shows up
and I have to close my phone down.

693
00:31:43,434 --> 00:31:44,702
Like, this is real.

694
00:31:44,702 --> 00:31:47,639
And one of the things
I love about Powerwash is

695
00:31:47,639 --> 00:31:50,692
I can literally walk in and play for 20 seconds
and then walk out, and it just

696
00:31:50,692 --> 00:31:52,577
is a game that has that.

697
00:31:52,577 --> 00:31:57,565
And what makes that possible in my mind
is miniature loops and major loops.

698
00:31:57,565 --> 00:32:01,219
So the miniature loop in Power Wash
is where you're cleaning the gunk off.

699
00:32:01,502 --> 00:32:04,138
And when you clean,
that feels super satisfying,

700
00:32:04,138 --> 00:32:06,357
and you're getting paid for it 
in that game.

701
00:32:06,357 --> 00:32:09,360
And so you're rewarded in many ways.

702
00:32:09,594 --> 00:32:10,979
And then there's major loops,

703
00:32:10,979 --> 00:32:13,982
which is when you finish a car
and the next car comes in.

704
00:32:14,632 --> 00:32:16,618
And there's an even what
I call a massive loop

705
00:32:16,618 --> 00:32:20,038
that I didn't put into the slide deck,
but a massive loop in Power Wash is that

706
00:32:20,271 --> 00:32:21,389
because the game is so—

707
00:32:21,389 --> 00:32:23,458
There's an end point,

708
00:32:24,742 --> 00:32:27,061
but you want people to keep playing,

709
00:32:27,061 --> 00:32:29,330
we built a prestige system in.
And if you haven't played

710
00:32:29,330 --> 00:32:32,333
a lot of mobile games that have prestige,
what that means is when

711
00:32:32,333 --> 00:32:36,321
you basically reach the end of the game,
you can reset back to level

712
00:32:36,321 --> 00:32:40,158
one and restart,
but you get a bonus multiplier.

713
00:32:40,408 --> 00:32:44,312
And so in the case of Power Wash,
you can prestige

714
00:32:44,462 --> 00:32:47,882
and double your money
that you're earning from level one.

715
00:32:47,882 --> 00:32:49,050
And you get to start all over again.

716
00:32:49,050 --> 00:32:51,753
And for people who want to seek that out,
that's a lot of fun.

717
00:32:51,753 --> 00:32:53,871
So we have miny, major,
and even massive loops

718
00:32:53,871 --> 00:32:55,757
if you want to like
literally reset the players.

719
00:32:55,757 --> 00:32:58,860
And I felt like we kind of had to in there.
If you don't have to, don't do it.

720
00:32:59,210 --> 00:33:02,163
But you definitely need mini
and major loops.

721
00:33:02,163 --> 00:33:04,432
Mini loops are the small fun, moment-
to-moment

722
00:33:04,432 --> 00:33:07,285
fun. And major loops are the 
thing that keeps you

723
00:33:07,285 --> 00:33:08,670
excited to keep playing.

724
00:33:09,721 --> 00:33:12,523
All right.

725
00:33:12,523 --> 00:33:13,224
I wanted to

726
00:33:13,224 --> 00:33:17,161
repeat this again because I think it's
so important: under 20 seconds.

727
00:33:17,161 --> 00:33:19,797
Like your target should be 10 seconds
to get to fun.

728
00:33:19,797 --> 00:33:23,935
People, I think, have felt like they saw
the 20 second number and said, “Oh yeah,”

729
00:33:23,935 --> 00:33:26,938
“I can make it 20 seconds in the beginning
before you get to the fun.”

730
00:33:26,938 --> 00:33:27,355
No, no, no.

731
00:33:27,355 --> 00:33:31,309
It's like, if you hit 20 seconds they're 
leaving your world.

732
00:33:31,492 --> 00:33:34,429
Or they start leaving your world
because it's too long. So you should

733
00:33:34,429 --> 00:33:37,432
try 10 seconds as your target.

734
00:33:37,432 --> 00:33:40,018
Like that would be my recommendation.

735
00:33:40,018 --> 00:33:44,038
The session length.
Whether we're talking about VR or mobile,

736
00:33:44,038 --> 00:33:47,358
I think five minutes should be like
your minimum target session length.

737
00:33:47,358 --> 00:33:50,578
So if you're thinking about an experience,
when I was developing Powerwash

738
00:33:50,578 --> 00:33:54,198
I thought about how long does it take
to clean the first car by yourself?

739
00:33:54,382 --> 00:33:56,818
And the time in my mind was five minutes.
It takes you five minutes.

740
00:33:56,818 --> 00:33:57,719
It's a long time,

741
00:33:57,719 --> 00:34:00,688
but there's miniature loops in there
on top of the miniature loop.

742
00:34:00,688 --> 00:34:02,457
So you have the mini
loop of cleaning each gunk,

743
00:34:02,457 --> 00:34:05,209
but then you can also upgrade
your power washer

744
00:34:05,209 --> 00:34:07,161
while you're in that first
five minute loop.

745
00:34:07,161 --> 00:34:10,164
And that's super satisfying
because it doubles up your speed.

746
00:34:10,431 --> 00:34:13,434
And that doubling up of speed
is a hooking algorithm.

747
00:34:13,434 --> 00:34:15,053
It's like a hook.

748
00:34:15,053 --> 00:34:18,172
When you start really slow and
it takes forever to clean a level one car,

749
00:34:18,289 --> 00:34:21,542
but then you go and level up your power 
washer by one level,

750
00:34:21,542 --> 00:34:25,730
and it literally doubles your speed
because you're damage goes from 1 to 2

751
00:34:26,130 --> 00:34:28,916
and you feel that. And it's so impactful.

752
00:34:28,916 --> 00:34:30,301
And then you go and do it a third time,

753
00:34:30,301 --> 00:34:33,171
it goes from 2 to 3,
which is a much smaller increment.

754
00:34:33,171 --> 00:34:34,072
It's not a double now.

755
00:34:34,072 --> 00:34:37,075
It's now it's just a 50% increase.

756
00:34:37,075 --> 00:34:40,678
That is still—
you're trying to get the same hit you got the

757
00:34:40,678 --> 00:34:41,846
first time, but you can't.

758
00:34:43,765 --> 00:34:46,768
It's engaging as waffle put it.

759
00:34:46,818 --> 00:34:49,036
I feel like I'm balancing a fine line
here.

760
00:34:49,036 --> 00:34:52,824
I'm like really nervous, to be honest,
as I develop what I consider to be

761
00:34:52,824 --> 00:34:54,292
addicting games.

762
00:34:54,292 --> 00:34:58,012
But, engaging is definitely
a more positive spin on that.

763
00:34:58,279 --> 00:35:03,084
But I do find myself actually being
almost negatively addicted to these games

764
00:35:03,084 --> 00:35:06,354
where I have to play and I can't leave,
and I have a hard time stopping.

765
00:35:06,587 --> 00:35:09,357
So the negative connotation
could be there.

766
00:35:09,357 --> 00:35:13,494
So, I have a moral dilemma there
that I'm kind of facing as I develop

767
00:35:14,028 --> 00:35:17,031
engaging, quote unquote, games.

768
00:35:18,416 --> 00:35:20,852
But yeah, so—
But there are a lot of fun, right?

769
00:35:20,852 --> 00:35:23,337
So it's a lot of fun. 5 to 15 minutes.

770
00:35:23,337 --> 00:35:27,258
So we start with making a game,
and you imagine your first major

771
00:35:27,258 --> 00:35:29,510
loop is a five minute loop.
At least that's what I did.

772
00:35:29,510 --> 00:35:32,780
And you can take your own spin on this
however you want to take a spin on it.

773
00:35:32,780 --> 00:35:35,817
But then you can imagine, 
“Oh, they play three of those.”

774
00:35:36,083 --> 00:35:37,218
That's 15 minutes, right?

775
00:35:38,703 --> 00:35:40,371
How do
you get them up to like a 15 minutes?

776
00:35:40,371 --> 00:35:42,140
If you can get them
up to a 15 minute average session—

777
00:35:42,140 --> 00:35:45,143
[...]

778
00:35:45,560 --> 00:35:47,612
And obviously mobile's
a little bit shorter session time,

779
00:35:47,612 --> 00:35:50,748
but when we're thinking about 15 minute
average session length for a VR

780
00:35:50,748 --> 00:35:53,751
user, that's a pretty good
chunk of their session time.

781
00:35:54,085 --> 00:35:58,339
At the Meta Connect Summit, we were told,
and they should be

782
00:35:58,339 --> 00:36:01,342
putting videos out with this,
so you can watch those soon too.

783
00:36:02,927 --> 00:36:05,096
But we were told that the average session
length

784
00:36:05,096 --> 00:36:07,849
is about 40, 45 minutes for VR users.

785
00:36:07,849 --> 00:36:10,468
So if you're taking up
15 minutes of their time in VR,

786
00:36:10,468 --> 00:36:12,303
you're taking up
a good majority of their time.

787
00:36:12,303 --> 00:36:17,208
And I know for the games that I play,
like Powerwash and Murder Manor and heck,

788
00:36:17,208 --> 00:36:20,328
even Royale back in the day,
those games we would spend hours in.

789
00:36:20,411 --> 00:36:22,680
And a lot of users
would be there for an hour.

790
00:36:22,680 --> 00:36:25,233
And so it was a prime spot.

791
00:36:25,233 --> 00:36:28,769
So we had a strong audience that really 
enjoyed that game

792
00:36:29,003 --> 00:36:29,971
and that was pretty awesome.

793
00:36:29,971 --> 00:36:32,807
Very, very rewarding as a creator even

794
00:36:32,807 --> 00:36:35,760
just like—
Yeah, it's pretty awesome to see that.

795
00:36:35,760 --> 00:36:36,861
But that's a good target.

796
00:36:36,861 --> 00:36:37,311
So like

797
00:36:37,311 --> 00:36:40,498
think about 15 minutes as a good target
to get people spending time in there.

798
00:36:40,982 --> 00:36:42,717
And then also think about those new users.

799
00:36:42,717 --> 00:36:44,402
Really,
really think about those new users.

800
00:36:44,402 --> 00:36:47,221
You really need to nail
their very first experience

801
00:36:47,221 --> 00:36:49,740
and make sure that it's amazing,
so that they actually end up

802
00:36:49,740 --> 00:36:52,910
staying for the first five minutes,
that they end up staying for those next

803
00:36:52,910 --> 00:36:53,778
15 minutes.

804
00:36:53,778 --> 00:36:56,547
And that they're coming back the next day
to not just spend 15 minutes,

805
00:36:56,547 --> 00:36:57,682
but to spend maybe an hour.

806
00:36:58,883 --> 00:37:00,902
And so
how do you do that is very difficult.

807
00:37:00,902 --> 00:37:03,738
It's like, okay, you got to get them
to the fun really fast.

808
00:37:03,738 --> 00:37:07,608
You've got to build something that rewards
them. Give them enough information

809
00:37:07,608 --> 00:37:10,611
that they've learned enough
on how to play the game.

810
00:37:11,362 --> 00:37:14,065
If you can socially engage them,

811
00:37:14,065 --> 00:37:16,367
so that they have some friends there
that they want to bring back—

812
00:37:16,367 --> 00:37:17,418
I don't know, there's a lot to it.

813
00:37:17,418 --> 00:37:20,488
But definitely think about it
as you're developing.

814
00:37:20,488 --> 00:37:23,157
And one of the ways that I do that,
personally, when I'm developing a world is

815
00:37:23,157 --> 00:37:25,643
I always think,
what's the very first experience,

816
00:37:25,643 --> 00:37:26,777
and I start there.

817
00:37:26,777 --> 00:37:28,346
I don't know
if this is the right way to do it.

818
00:37:28,346 --> 00:37:31,849
You should probably have an idea
of what the full

819
00:37:32,099 --> 00:37:35,253
user experience is from the beginning.
But I always start at the very beginning,

820
00:37:35,253 --> 00:37:39,023
and then I tweak it. And I keep feeling it
out. Like I use a little bit of my own

821
00:37:39,023 --> 00:37:43,010
gut and intuition to be like, “Okay,
what is this like as a brand new user?”

822
00:37:43,010 --> 00:37:46,247
I load into the world.
So I go into preview mode, I walk in,

823
00:37:46,380 --> 00:37:48,149
and I try to just imagine
being a brand new user.

824
00:37:48,149 --> 00:37:51,719
And I do that over, and over, and over again
every single time.

825
00:37:51,719 --> 00:37:55,289
And even in my my scripts in TypeScript,
I've got a button that I can press

826
00:37:55,289 --> 00:37:58,292
that resets me back
to being like day zero.

827
00:37:58,309 --> 00:38:02,313
So every time I can try
testing it as a new user.

828
00:38:03,331 --> 00:38:04,081
So building

829
00:38:04,081 --> 00:38:07,285
debugging tools and such like that
is really, really useful.

830
00:38:08,269 --> 00:38:10,588
But definitely a higher level topic.

831
00:38:10,588 --> 00:38:13,074
But point is, really refining

832
00:38:13,074 --> 00:38:16,527
that very first experience
and reliving it over and over again.

833
00:38:16,527 --> 00:38:19,764
But then, the other thing that we did
with Power Wash that I think worked out

834
00:38:19,764 --> 00:38:22,316
pretty well for us
is we kept bringing in new testers

835
00:38:22,316 --> 00:38:24,618
so that we could see
what that first experience was like.

836
00:38:24,618 --> 00:38:27,888
So the first time we brought in testers,
we had a button that would spawn the car

837
00:38:27,888 --> 00:38:30,541
in, and we found that
that was a bad experience.

838
00:38:30,541 --> 00:38:32,610
People couldn't figure out the button,
they were confused.

839
00:38:32,610 --> 00:38:34,829
It was too much to try and figure out
right off the bat,

840
00:38:34,829 --> 00:38:38,032
even with like text and pop-ups
and all the things that you could possibly

841
00:38:38,032 --> 00:38:38,883
do to make that button

842
00:38:39,867 --> 00:38:40,651
more visible.

843
00:38:40,651 --> 00:38:42,720
So we had to make sure it happened
automatically.

844
00:38:42,720 --> 00:38:45,723
You walked in
and then poof, the thing happened.

845
00:38:46,007 --> 00:38:47,958
And we kept bringing in new users
to test and

846
00:38:47,958 --> 00:38:49,310
figure out what was the next problem.

847
00:38:49,310 --> 00:38:52,613
And we learned, “Oh, they can't figure out
what to do because it wasn't very clear.”

848
00:38:52,613 --> 00:38:56,701
So we start adding like exclamations
above the thing and make it as clear

849
00:38:56,701 --> 00:38:58,319
as we possibly can.

850
00:38:58,319 --> 00:39:01,322
So bringing
people in to test is very important.

851
00:39:01,372 --> 00:39:05,009
And making sure that that initial user
experience is what you expect it to be

852
00:39:05,009 --> 00:39:08,696
because there's often times that
when you're playing it, you miss—

853
00:39:08,696 --> 00:39:11,699
Things that are obvious to you are just completely
not obvious to new users.

854
00:39:12,266 --> 00:39:13,818
Okay.

855
00:39:13,818 --> 00:39:16,470
Continuing on the algorithm
side of things. Things

856
00:39:16,470 --> 00:39:19,340
that Horizon is looking for.
We know that we have to get people, users

857
00:39:19,340 --> 00:39:21,625
to return within seven days,
or at least I hope you know this.

858
00:39:21,625 --> 00:39:25,563
And if you don't, take a note.
When a user first visits,

859
00:39:26,130 --> 00:39:28,733
they need to visit the next day
through the next

860
00:39:28,733 --> 00:39:31,769
seven days for that to be considered
like a retained user.

861
00:39:32,002 --> 00:39:34,805
But then there's this kind of larger
algorithm in Horizon

862
00:39:34,805 --> 00:39:36,507
that's looking at the monthly version
of this. 

863
00:39:36,507 --> 00:39:40,077
Did they return for the next week,
and the next week, and the next week?

864
00:39:40,344 --> 00:39:42,747
And so on, and for at least four weeks.

865
00:39:42,747 --> 00:39:44,698
And so when you're
really thinking about like,

866
00:39:44,698 --> 00:39:46,467
“Oh, how do I get this to be like something”

867
00:39:46,467 --> 00:39:49,253
“that Meta will pick up
and start promoting?”

868
00:39:49,253 --> 00:39:50,671
This is the numbers they're looking at.

869
00:39:50,671 --> 00:39:52,673
They're looking to see are your users,

870
00:39:52,673 --> 00:39:56,761
a good percentage of your users
retaining for months? Not just days,

871
00:39:56,761 --> 00:39:59,997
not just a week,
but actually like monthly.

872
00:40:00,748 --> 00:40:02,750
So be thinking about that
as you're developing.

873
00:40:02,750 --> 00:40:04,652
And one of the biggest downsides
to Power Wash is

874
00:40:04,652 --> 00:40:06,470
I really believe it's a two week game.

875
00:40:06,470 --> 00:40:08,939
I do not think it's a four week game.
And it's one thing that we're

876
00:40:08,939 --> 00:40:11,942
trying to figure out how do we make it
so that you can play it for longer?

877
00:40:12,626 --> 00:40:14,428
Because right now
the game ends after two weeks.

878
00:40:14,428 --> 00:40:16,397
You've basically
done everything you can do.

879
00:40:17,665 --> 00:40:19,283
And that kind of stinks.

880
00:40:19,283 --> 00:40:21,535
I don't hate it.
I still love the game.

881
00:40:21,535 --> 00:40:23,988
I put a lot of fun into it,
but I might go work on the next project

882
00:40:23,988 --> 00:40:26,424
and just leave it where it's at
and be like, “Oh, that's okay.”

883
00:40:26,424 --> 00:40:27,408
It doesn't hit that target,

884
00:40:27,408 --> 00:40:29,577
but we did our best.

885
00:40:29,577 --> 00:40:31,579
Next up: Progressive depth.

886
00:40:31,579 --> 00:40:33,647
So we talked a lot about depth here
and there.

887
00:40:33,647 --> 00:40:36,267
But let's like really dive into it.
So here's a graph.

888
00:40:36,267 --> 00:40:38,569
This is a similar graph to the one
represented at the summit,

889
00:40:38,569 --> 00:40:40,821
and you'll see that in one of those talks,
like I mentioned.

890
00:40:40,821 --> 00:40:44,341
And what you have here on the left is the
number of visitors visiting your world.

891
00:40:44,642 --> 00:40:46,977
And this is how much knowledge they have.

892
00:40:46,977 --> 00:40:51,198
So this means
if you were the only visitor to your world

893
00:40:51,198 --> 00:40:54,919
and you're the creator,
you have 100% depth of knowledge.

894
00:40:55,169 --> 00:40:58,939
The second you add a second visitor,
you can share a lot of knowledge

895
00:40:58,939 --> 00:41:00,624
with them,
but you can't give them everything.

896
00:41:00,624 --> 00:41:03,177
You can do your best,
but it's going to be slightly less.

897
00:41:03,177 --> 00:41:07,731
And as you grow your users to the point
where Meta is pushing out

898
00:41:07,998 --> 00:41:10,801
this to the world, and everybody's seeing it, 
and everybody’s jumping

899
00:41:10,801 --> 00:41:12,570
in. And you can advertise
your own world yourself too.

900
00:41:12,570 --> 00:41:14,722
It doesn't have to be by Meta,
but point is

901
00:41:14,722 --> 00:41:18,058
if your world's getting advertised
and tons of new users are showing up,

902
00:41:20,127 --> 00:41:22,346
suddenly there's no depth of knowledge.

903
00:41:22,346 --> 00:41:24,048
Nobody knows anything about your game.

904
00:41:24,048 --> 00:41:26,634
They're showing up with actually
no knowledge whatsoever,

905
00:41:26,634 --> 00:41:29,637
and they’ve got to
still have a good experience.

906
00:41:29,937 --> 00:41:33,240
And so I think a lot of us,
and I fully admit to being in

907
00:41:33,240 --> 00:41:36,527
this, are down here a little bit
where we've got a lot of users

908
00:41:36,527 --> 00:41:39,630
who know our game
very well, and people are showing up

909
00:41:39,830 --> 00:41:40,915
and they're new users,

910
00:41:40,915 --> 00:41:43,951
but they're showing up in an instance
with people who do know the game.

911
00:41:44,268 --> 00:41:47,071
And those people are really what continues

912
00:41:47,071 --> 00:41:50,057
training those new visitors
and teaching them how to play.

913
00:41:50,658 --> 00:41:52,793
And so that's okay.

914
00:41:52,793 --> 00:41:55,312
But it's going to prevent you
from getting to here.

915
00:41:55,312 --> 00:41:58,132
Like if your game doesn't
have the ability to be played—

916
00:41:58,132 --> 00:42:00,801
Or your world, whether it's a game
or not, this doesn't really matter.

917
00:42:02,836 --> 00:42:05,039
They need to be able

918
00:42:05,039 --> 00:42:08,092
to enjoy the experience
the moment they arrive with

919
00:42:08,092 --> 00:42:11,545
nobody—like you need to show up in a world
that has zero people in there

920
00:42:11,545 --> 00:42:12,897
who've ever played your world,

921
00:42:12,897 --> 00:42:14,815
and they need to be able
to have a good time.

922
00:42:14,815 --> 00:42:16,050
And then they come back with that

923
00:42:16,050 --> 00:42:19,486
same group of people let’s say, the next day—
their depth is going to grow.

924
00:42:19,820 --> 00:42:21,505
And so I call this like—

925
00:42:21,505 --> 00:42:24,542
I don't know, I don't have a really good
name for it. But progressive depth, right?

926
00:42:25,142 --> 00:42:27,228
Over time,
the longer they play, the more they come

927
00:42:27,228 --> 00:42:29,330
back, the larger
their depth is going to be.

928
00:42:29,330 --> 00:42:32,366
And so the focus in my mind
when I think about depth of knowledge

929
00:42:32,583 --> 00:42:36,053
is not to have to require them
to have a bunch of knowledge.

930
00:42:36,053 --> 00:42:36,804
It's not to limit

931
00:42:36,804 --> 00:42:40,291
the amount of depth of knowledge. It's
to see them as coming in with nothing,

932
00:42:40,524 --> 00:42:43,561
and every day they continue playing
that knowledge grows a little bit

933
00:42:43,561 --> 00:42:44,628
more, and a little bit more.

934
00:42:46,163 --> 00:42:49,166
So that's the idea in my mind.

935
00:42:49,350 --> 00:42:50,601
More visitors equals less stuff.

936
00:42:50,601 --> 00:42:51,986
We mentioned that. Little depth required.

937
00:42:51,986 --> 00:42:54,355
We mentioned that. Retain the users
and grow their depth.

938
00:42:54,355 --> 00:42:56,190
That's really
what I think is most important.

939
00:42:56,190 --> 00:42:58,259
Retain the users, grow their depth.

940
00:42:58,259 --> 00:43:01,912
Don't think about it as like you can't
build a fun, engaging, deep game.

941
00:43:02,429 --> 00:43:05,549
Build it
so that they don't have to be deep,

942
00:43:05,699 --> 00:43:07,901
but they can get there
the longer they play.

943
00:43:07,901 --> 00:43:12,089
And I feel like that 
really is an amazing thing to provide

944
00:43:12,089 --> 00:43:12,573
to users.

945
00:43:12,573 --> 00:43:15,559
If you can really give them more
to explore and discover—

946
00:43:15,960 --> 00:43:18,862
I think a good example of this
is actually one of

947
00:43:18,862 --> 00:43:21,865
my good friends,

948
00:43:22,082 --> 00:43:22,650
MetaJorgen.

949
00:43:22,650 --> 00:43:26,570
He has a world
where he put the most complicated mechanic

950
00:43:26,570 --> 00:43:30,207
behind the building
you spawn in, so you will not discover it

951
00:43:30,324 --> 00:43:33,927
until you've been playing for a long time.
Because nobody looks behind the building

952
00:43:33,927 --> 00:43:37,014
you spawn in.
It's such a hard place to discover,

953
00:43:37,014 --> 00:43:40,351
but when you do discover it, it's
one of the most incredible mechanics.

954
00:43:42,086 --> 00:43:43,020
I forgot the name of the world.

955
00:43:43,020 --> 00:43:44,838
It's like Lost in the Wild by Meta

956
00:43:44,838 --> 00:43:48,092
Jorgen. It's an exploration world
with asset spawning.

957
00:43:48,092 --> 00:43:49,376
It's really cool.

958
00:43:49,376 --> 00:43:49,710
And the—

959
00:43:49,710 --> 00:43:52,946
It’s a crafting table behind
the building just for reference.

960
00:43:52,946 --> 00:43:54,565
Yeah, so super cool.

961
00:43:54,565 --> 00:43:57,801
Okay, so continuing on in our talk—
recommendations.

962
00:44:00,204 --> 00:44:02,940
It is highly recommended that you learn
and apply the following things.

963
00:44:02,940 --> 00:44:03,857
And I want to just before

964
00:44:03,857 --> 00:44:06,860
we talk about what these things are,
explain a little bit of why.

965
00:44:08,095 --> 00:44:10,381
What I saw—this is like,

966
00:44:10,381 --> 00:44:13,133
I guess, my opinion.

967
00:44:13,133 --> 00:44:16,136
At Connect, the last two summits—

968
00:44:16,520 --> 00:44:19,757
This year, previous year to that.

969
00:44:19,790 --> 00:44:21,508
Let's go back to the very beginning.

970
00:44:21,508 --> 00:44:24,511
The first summit.
They told us very explicitly,

971
00:44:24,511 --> 00:44:27,498
TypeScript is the future. Custom Model
Import is the future.

972
00:44:27,564 --> 00:44:30,467
And without being like, “Do this.”

973
00:44:30,467 --> 00:44:33,420
Without overly saying,
“Do this. Do this. Do this.”

974
00:44:33,420 --> 00:44:35,572
It was pretty clear we had to do this.

975
00:44:35,572 --> 00:44:38,842
And so, I had to immediately
start transitioning—

976
00:44:38,842 --> 00:44:41,845
Like we built Murder Manor
originally in Code Blocks.

977
00:44:42,112 --> 00:44:43,497
And I think we're going to just do

978
00:44:43,497 --> 00:44:46,500
Custom Model Import,
but they're like—anyway, the point is

979
00:44:47,251 --> 00:44:50,037
it was our very first world
using both TypeScript

980
00:44:50,037 --> 00:44:53,207
and Custom Model Import, but it was like
we were told we have to do this.

981
00:44:54,508 --> 00:44:55,409
And so I want you to

982
00:44:55,409 --> 00:44:58,412
hear the “Do this” for this last year.

983
00:44:58,529 --> 00:45:01,348
And what we saw at this Summit
this year, in my opinion,

984
00:45:01,348 --> 00:45:04,501
was mostly people invited who were using
TypeScript, who were using Custom

985
00:45:04,501 --> 00:45:07,488
Model Import,
or who at least dabbled in it.

986
00:45:07,554 --> 00:45:10,974
And so this year we were told,
“We need you to do mobile development.”

987
00:45:10,974 --> 00:45:12,543
“We need you to do in-world purchases.”

988
00:45:12,543 --> 00:45:14,628
And without saying overly “Do, do, do.”

989
00:45:14,628 --> 00:45:17,631
We were basically told, “Do these two things.”

990
00:45:17,664 --> 00:45:20,417
And so if you want to do this for more

991
00:45:20,417 --> 00:45:23,987
than just fun, you really need to take
these recommendations

992
00:45:23,987 --> 00:45:25,139
from Meta seriously.

993
00:45:25,139 --> 00:45:28,592
We got to look at what Meta is targeting,
what their direction is.

994
00:45:29,009 --> 00:45:31,795
And as much as I love Code Blocks,
because I really do,

995
00:45:31,795 --> 00:45:34,498
I love the fact that it makes it easy
for everyone to create.

996
00:45:34,498 --> 00:45:38,869
I also recognize that it's
not as heavily supported.

997
00:45:38,869 --> 00:45:41,872
I hope we can continue pushing on them
to keep it supported forever.

998
00:45:42,689 --> 00:45:45,809
But it definitely, like,
we just saw weeks of it

999
00:45:45,809 --> 00:45:48,812
being broken and that
would never happen in TypeScript.

1000
00:45:49,580 --> 00:45:52,232
So we need to keep vocalizing
that we need them to fix it,

1001
00:45:52,232 --> 00:45:53,784
absolutely. But,

1002
00:45:55,252 --> 00:45:56,754
we also should be recognizing

1003
00:45:56,754 --> 00:45:58,939
where their priorities are
and potentially shifting,

1004
00:45:58,939 --> 00:46:01,308
especially if you feel like
you want to do it

1005
00:46:01,308 --> 00:46:04,311
for the long term. For the,
let's be honest, the money.

1006
00:46:04,795 --> 00:46:07,548
And you really want to grow with Meta
in that regard.

1007
00:46:07,548 --> 00:46:11,001
So, mobile world
development is obviously their priority.

1008
00:46:11,268 --> 00:46:14,455
And we talked a little bit
about being mobile-first versus VR-first.

1009
00:46:14,455 --> 00:46:16,740
And I think you can choose either/or. 
I don't really care.

1010
00:46:16,740 --> 00:46:18,559
But what I really care about is that

1011
00:46:18,559 --> 00:46:21,562
if you do choose VR-first,
you think about supporting mobile.

1012
00:46:21,562 --> 00:46:25,532
I just went to a world a couple days ago
and it was VR-

1013
00:46:25,532 --> 00:46:27,801
only to the point
where you couldn't get in the entrance

1014
00:46:27,801 --> 00:46:30,420
unless you could open your
controllers apart.

1015
00:46:30,420 --> 00:46:31,822
And so, you couldn't even go into—

1016
00:46:31,822 --> 00:46:34,825
Because I was just going in on mobile
to demonstrate what was in there,

1017
00:46:34,975 --> 00:46:35,809
but I couldn't do that.

1018
00:46:37,528 --> 00:46:39,480
And like, just don't put blockers
in!

1019
00:46:39,480 --> 00:46:42,900
At least let them in. Even if it's
not a great experience on mobile,

1020
00:46:42,900 --> 00:46:45,903
at least let them in
to come and play and hang out.

1021
00:46:46,019 --> 00:46:49,022
And one of my favorite experiences
of this most recently is

1022
00:46:49,673 --> 00:46:51,175
being in Power Wash, developing it.

1023
00:46:51,175 --> 00:46:54,978
I would go in VR. My headset would die.

1024
00:46:54,978 --> 00:46:56,780
Then I’d pick up my phone and I’d jump back in

1025
00:46:56,780 --> 00:46:58,398
and be able to continue the conversation.

1026
00:46:58,398 --> 00:47:02,636
And I feel like that's
the dream universe for this

1027
00:47:02,636 --> 00:47:05,989
mobile and VR, 
where you can pick up immediately.

1028
00:47:05,989 --> 00:47:09,109
Your headset dies, or the opposite—
you're on your phone, you get home

1029
00:47:09,359 --> 00:47:12,362
and you want to continue
picking up in VR and you can do that.

1030
00:47:12,663 --> 00:47:15,199
So like imagining a kid in the back seat,
right?

1031
00:47:15,199 --> 00:47:17,334
You're not playing on your phone
while you're driving.

1032
00:47:17,334 --> 00:47:18,752
Maybe you're on a bus.

1033
00:47:18,752 --> 00:47:19,970
I don't know. There's scenarios

1034
00:47:19,970 --> 00:47:21,688
there. Point is—

1035
00:47:21,688 --> 00:47:24,808
you want to be seeing your users
going from mobile to VR, back to mobile,

1036
00:47:25,042 --> 00:47:25,792
and vice versa.

1037
00:47:26,927 --> 00:47:29,179
So mobile-first to me means

1038
00:47:29,179 --> 00:47:32,633
that you're really putting an emphasis
on making it an amazing mobile experience,

1039
00:47:32,633 --> 00:47:34,968
but if you come into VR it's even better.

1040
00:47:34,968 --> 00:47:37,971
And if you're VR-first, it's playable 
on mobile.

1041
00:47:38,088 --> 00:47:41,358
We didn't prioritize you, but
at least you can come in and play.

1042
00:47:42,593 --> 00:47:47,431
So, definitely think
about that ability to go back and forth

1043
00:47:47,431 --> 00:47:50,934
and make sure you're developing for mobile, 
at least on the minor level.

1044
00:47:52,019 --> 00:47:54,888
In-World Purchases. This is,
like I mentioned, very huge.

1045
00:47:54,888 --> 00:47:59,660
I feel so much better about it
than I did a year ago. Two years ago even.

1046
00:48:00,027 --> 00:48:03,864
The point is, to me, in-world
purchases for so long have been kids

1047
00:48:03,864 --> 00:48:08,118
asking their parents to use their credit
card to buy X, Y, or Z.

1048
00:48:08,118 --> 00:48:10,220
Insert your in-world purchase here.

1049
00:48:10,220 --> 00:48:15,859
And that never felt good to me.
But what I can feel good about

1050
00:48:15,859 --> 00:48:18,145
is the new credit system
that Meta is working on,

1051
00:48:18,145 --> 00:48:20,964
because what we see
there is this potential for kids

1052
00:48:20,964 --> 00:48:22,766
to be given monthly allowances.

1053
00:48:22,766 --> 00:48:26,270
They can—and I feel like to me
that's going to be huge

1054
00:48:26,270 --> 00:48:26,987
because I don't have to

1055
00:48:26,987 --> 00:48:30,807
feel bad about saying, “Hey, here's
this thing you can buy for X credits.”

1056
00:48:30,958 --> 00:48:31,642
Meta credits.

1057
00:48:33,527 --> 00:48:37,014
And that therefore removes

1058
00:48:37,014 --> 00:48:41,518
that sort of mental blocker for me,
and makes it a lot more easy to do.

1059
00:48:41,518 --> 00:48:44,838
And I have family,
some nieces, who already get that.

1060
00:48:44,838 --> 00:48:47,941
They get $20 a month in Roblox. $20
a month in

1061
00:48:49,076 --> 00:48:50,777
Fortnite, and so on.

1062
00:48:50,777 --> 00:48:53,880
And so I think in a year to two years,
we're going to see that normalize,

1063
00:48:53,880 --> 00:48:55,766
where people who are VR

1064
00:48:55,766 --> 00:48:59,519
regular kid players are going to be asking
their parents for Christmas next year.

1065
00:48:59,703 --> 00:49:02,689
“Hey, can I get some Meta
credits? Gift cards?”

1066
00:49:03,440 --> 00:49:04,074
And we'll see

1067
00:49:04,074 --> 00:49:06,059
the grandparents
start doing that in a couple of years.

1068
00:49:06,059 --> 00:49:07,711
I’m very excited about that

1069
00:49:07,711 --> 00:49:09,329
because I think for us,
this means we're going to able

1070
00:49:09,329 --> 00:49:12,082
to make a ton more money
than we've ever been able to make before.

1071
00:49:13,884 --> 00:49:16,253
And so, the two things that we've been told

1072
00:49:16,253 --> 00:49:20,357
are the most successful in present day
Horizon are currency and power-ups.

1073
00:49:20,357 --> 00:49:24,061
So in-world currency is being able
to exchange real world money for in-world

1074
00:49:24,061 --> 00:49:28,398
currencies is something that people love
doing. And it's very successful.

1075
00:49:28,682 --> 00:49:31,401
And power-ups would be thinking 
about Mythioses

1076
00:49:31,401 --> 00:49:33,470
racing game
where you can buy a speed boost.

1077
00:49:33,470 --> 00:49:36,473
And so you can buy a bunch of speed boosts
and get going,

1078
00:49:37,641 --> 00:49:38,241
and so on.

1079
00:49:38,241 --> 00:49:40,761
So those are the two things
that are currently most successful.

1080
00:49:40,761 --> 00:49:42,829
I will note that
I think the most important thing

1081
00:49:42,829 --> 00:49:45,349
about in-world purchases
is that they're not one-off purchases.

1082
00:49:45,349 --> 00:49:47,401
They're kind of like—think about
how can you make sure

1083
00:49:47,401 --> 00:49:50,387
that they can keep making that purchase
over, and over, and over again?

1084
00:49:50,971 --> 00:49:52,439
For better or worse.

1085
00:49:52,439 --> 00:49:56,877
But if you do one-time purchases,
you can't continue doing that, right?

1086
00:49:56,877 --> 00:50:00,831
So a one-time purchase means that
it's already been bought.

1087
00:50:00,981 --> 00:50:03,050
There's nothing else for them to buy.

1088
00:50:03,050 --> 00:50:05,602
So, if you have users who could spend $100,

1089
00:50:05,602 --> 00:50:09,272
but you only have one $10 item,
you have missed that opportunity.

1090
00:50:10,741 --> 00:50:12,359
So just—

1091
00:50:12,359 --> 00:50:15,328
Think about that as you're developing
your in-world purchases.

1092
00:50:15,328 --> 00:50:18,331
TypeScript is amazingly powerful.

1093
00:50:18,632 --> 00:50:21,568
Once you get into TypeScript,
you can easily

1094
00:50:21,568 --> 00:50:25,022
develop potentially even faster
than you can develop Code Blocks.

1095
00:50:25,022 --> 00:50:27,174
It definitely has a steep learning curve,

1096
00:50:27,174 --> 00:50:28,942
especially if you don't have any coding
background.

1097
00:50:28,942 --> 00:50:30,894
I had a mild coding background.

1098
00:50:30,894 --> 00:50:33,864
I mostly knew how to 
write code that I'd seen other people write,

1099
00:50:33,864 --> 00:50:36,717
but I was able to pick it up.
I mean, I guess I was learning.

1100
00:50:36,717 --> 00:50:41,455
I followed a Unity tutorial series earlier
in the last year about

1101
00:50:41,872 --> 00:50:42,873
developing for C Sharp,

1102
00:50:42,873 --> 00:50:46,159
and that was like probably
the best kickstart into TypeScript for me.

1103
00:50:46,860 --> 00:50:48,628
But it is so much different than C Sharp.

1104
00:50:48,628 --> 00:50:51,331
So definitely, if you're gonna dive into
TypeScript, just dive into TypeScript.

1105
00:50:53,250 --> 00:50:55,435
But the point is: it is hard.

1106
00:50:55,435 --> 00:50:56,837
It's not easy.

1107
00:50:56,837 --> 00:50:59,306
Like, I had some coding background
in TypeScript.

1108
00:50:59,306 --> 00:51:02,309
It took me two months to get a firm grasp of.

1109
00:51:02,309 --> 00:51:05,629
And then even after that,
I spent this entire year developing that.

1110
00:51:05,629 --> 00:51:07,531
And still there's
so much more to learn.

1111
00:51:07,531 --> 00:51:10,217
And I'm still just barely,

1112
00:51:10,217 --> 00:51:11,701
barely what I'd say competent.

1113
00:51:11,701 --> 00:51:16,039
There's still so much more
to grasp at and uncover.

1114
00:51:16,039 --> 00:51:18,892
So—but that doesn't mean you can't start.

1115
00:51:18,892 --> 00:51:23,180
So for me, I think learning TypeScript
means seeing action happen.

1116
00:51:23,396 --> 00:51:25,165
So I just recently posted a tutorial.

1117
00:51:25,165 --> 00:51:27,534
You can check it out
in the tutorial section off the Discord.

1118
00:51:27,534 --> 00:51:29,419
And then last up is Custom Model Import.

1119
00:51:29,419 --> 00:51:32,789
Another really awesome one,
but I feel like it's very easy to assume

1120
00:51:33,023 --> 00:51:35,942
that you have to build all the 3D models
yourself.

1121
00:51:35,942 --> 00:51:37,127
That is not the case.

1122
00:51:37,127 --> 00:51:39,529
If you are 3D modeler and that's
what you want to do, absolutely.

1123
00:51:39,529 --> 00:51:42,048
By all means be a 3D modeler.

1124
00:51:42,048 --> 00:51:45,051
Do what you enjoy is my mantra in life.

1125
00:51:45,602 --> 00:51:47,988
As best as you can, of course, but the—

1126
00:51:47,988 --> 00:51:50,690
Don't be a murderer.
Don't enjoy murder. Okay?

1127
00:51:50,690 --> 00:51:53,043
Just do it in Murder Manor.
Do it in Murder Village.

1128
00:51:53,043 --> 00:51:54,744
Or is this—
Don’t do it in real life!

1129
00:51:56,163 --> 00:51:57,147
This is not legal advice.

1130
00:51:57,147 --> 00:51:57,747
Consult a lawyer.

1131
00:51:57,747 --> 00:51:59,633
I don't know, I'm
losing my mind right now!

1132
00:51:59,633 --> 00:52:02,519
Point is: do what you enjoy in life.

1133
00:52:02,519 --> 00:52:05,122
[Laughs]
With some limitations.

1134
00:52:05,122 --> 00:52:10,076
Woo! Anyhoo...
Custom Model Import.

1135
00:52:10,076 --> 00:52:12,479
If you enjoy it, do it.

1136
00:52:12,479 --> 00:52:14,247
But there's this, like, separating.

1137
00:52:14,247 --> 00:52:16,766
I've focused my entire year
on learning coding.

1138
00:52:16,766 --> 00:52:19,769
I've had to learn a little bit of Custom
Model Import, but

1139
00:52:20,787 --> 00:52:23,657
it's better for me
to work with other creators

1140
00:52:23,657 --> 00:52:26,660
that do Custom Model Import.
Like that's their thing.

1141
00:52:26,660 --> 00:52:30,564
And so, it's going to be a good time
to start networking,

1142
00:52:30,564 --> 00:52:33,266
connecting with people
and trading services.

1143
00:52:33,266 --> 00:52:34,951
Whether that's, “Hey,
you helped me with mine

1144
00:52:34,951 --> 00:52:37,938
and I'll help you with yours.”
Or however you want to work that out.

1145
00:52:38,522 --> 00:52:41,391
But definitely, you don't have to do
Custom Model Import.

1146
00:52:41,391 --> 00:52:43,260
You can buy Unity assets.

1147
00:52:43,260 --> 00:52:46,379
There's whole packages of asset sets.

1148
00:52:46,580 --> 00:52:48,331
I think the biggest thing

1149
00:52:48,331 --> 00:52:52,352
that I've heard that I agree with
is being careful not to buy too

1150
00:52:53,303 --> 00:52:55,739
separated themes.

1151
00:52:55,739 --> 00:52:58,041
Right? Like styles.
You want your styles to match.

1152
00:52:58,041 --> 00:53:00,293
If your styles don't match,

1153
00:53:00,293 --> 00:53:01,828
it's just not going to look very good.

1154
00:53:01,828 --> 00:53:05,282
So you really want to try to make sure
the styling of the assets

1155
00:53:05,282 --> 00:53:08,268
you're buying matches
the overall styling of your world.

1156
00:53:08,752 --> 00:53:11,538
So think about that.

1157
00:53:11,538 --> 00:53:13,173
But yeah, if you're a scripter

1158
00:53:13,173 --> 00:53:16,643
and you don't want to learn 3D modeling,
you can do that.

1159
00:53:16,643 --> 00:53:18,778
There is some solutions there.

1160
00:53:18,778 --> 00:53:21,448
Next up, we've got takeaways!

1161
00:53:21,448 --> 00:53:21,932
Remember—

1162
00:53:21,932 --> 00:53:25,886
So this is just a quick recap
of the whole talk. Effects & Action.

1163
00:53:25,886 --> 00:53:28,822
This is important.
Users need to create action in the world.

1164
00:53:28,822 --> 00:53:32,659
They want to feel effects—visual effects,
sound effects, haptic feedback.

1165
00:53:32,659 --> 00:53:34,461
They want to make—

1166
00:53:34,461 --> 00:53:37,230
Give them something! Make them
feel something in some sort of way.

1167
00:53:37,230 --> 00:53:39,749
When they do something,
it causes something to happen.

1168
00:53:39,749 --> 00:53:41,101
You need mini and major loops.

1169
00:53:41,101 --> 00:53:43,370
So things that are really small
that are satisfying,

1170
00:53:43,370 --> 00:53:47,357
but major loops that are also satisfying
and engaging, so to speak.

1171
00:53:47,724 --> 00:53:51,294
Social Creativity: allow users
to define the experience.

1172
00:53:51,294 --> 00:53:52,345
Don't overly,

1173
00:53:53,546 --> 00:53:54,598
[...]

1174
00:53:54,598 --> 00:53:58,969
Don't be too strict
on what the experience is. 

1175
00:53:59,269 --> 00:54:01,972
I had an example
I was going to share, which was

1176
00:54:01,972 --> 00:54:05,425
if you're building a card game,
and the card game is—

1177
00:54:06,543 --> 00:54:09,179
You don't want anyone else
to see the cards, so you make it

1178
00:54:09,179 --> 00:54:12,332
so only the user sitting at that booth
can see their cards.

1179
00:54:12,866 --> 00:54:14,267
But what about the people who want to walk

1180
00:54:14,267 --> 00:54:17,487
around the table and
engage and see what everyone's got?

1181
00:54:17,487 --> 00:54:21,775
Like a real world would be like?
Maybe that extra effort to make it

1182
00:54:21,775 --> 00:54:24,828
so only one user can see
it is actually ruining the experience.

1183
00:54:24,828 --> 00:54:26,997
Sure,
you could make it so that only that user

1184
00:54:26,997 --> 00:54:29,849
and the people not playing could see it.
That's an option.

1185
00:54:29,849 --> 00:54:31,618
But you might be thinking, “Oh,
but what if that person”

1186
00:54:31,618 --> 00:54:34,621
“who's looking over the shoulder
tells everybody what the cards are?”

1187
00:54:34,821 --> 00:54:36,506
That's a fun social experience.

1188
00:54:36,506 --> 00:54:39,175
As much as that quote ruins
the experience,

1189
00:54:39,175 --> 00:54:42,178
it actually create something unique
that you wouldn't have otherwise had,

1190
00:54:42,295 --> 00:54:46,383
which makes it more fun.
Because, “Oh! We can talk bad.”

1191
00:54:46,383 --> 00:54:46,766
We can talk

1192
00:54:46,766 --> 00:54:50,570
about how that affected the gameplay,
and how that made the outcomes different.

1193
00:54:51,988 --> 00:54:54,057
Social creativity is huge.

1194
00:54:54,057 --> 00:54:56,393
Don't overly regulate.
That's what I would say.

1195
00:54:56,393 --> 00:55:00,330
So, give them some freedom
to be creative.

1196
00:55:00,447 --> 00:55:02,399
Randomness can turn into retention, guys.

1197
00:55:02,399 --> 00:55:03,984
We talked a lot about that
at the beginning, right?

1198
00:55:03,984 --> 00:55:07,103
So random roles, random items—
expecting random.

1199
00:55:07,103 --> 00:55:09,155
I feel like
this is a little like loot boxes.

1200
00:55:09,155 --> 00:55:10,940
So I feel a little nervous

1201
00:55:10,940 --> 00:55:14,878
building randomness in like this,
but it is like a loot box.

1202
00:55:14,878 --> 00:55:16,730
It's really exciting to see.
“What are you going to get?”

1203
00:55:16,730 --> 00:55:18,331
“What are you going to get?”

1204
00:55:18,331 --> 00:55:21,601
Whether it's a role, or an item,
or anything else.

1205
00:55:22,669 --> 00:55:23,520
Keep it simple.

1206
00:55:23,520 --> 00:55:27,540
I think one of the biggest things that 
cannot be overstated

1207
00:55:27,540 --> 00:55:29,943
is the fact that it's very easy
to make things

1208
00:55:29,943 --> 00:55:33,146
that are overly complicated. But keeping
it simple is the most important thing.

1209
00:55:33,747 --> 00:55:37,650
And find the fun in that simple thing.
Find that fun really early

1210
00:55:37,851 --> 00:55:40,253
and then focus in on that.
Try not to lose it.

1211
00:55:40,253 --> 00:55:42,722
If you do lose it, go back and see
how can we bring it back?

1212
00:55:42,722 --> 00:55:44,207
How can we like get back to the fun?

1213
00:55:45,542 --> 00:55:47,160
And keeping it simple can help
you do that.

1214
00:55:47,160 --> 00:55:49,729
And also, we've only got one month
for this current competition,

1215
00:55:49,729 --> 00:55:52,949
which is a great exercise
whether or not you win or not.

1216
00:55:52,949 --> 00:55:57,320
It's a great exercise in building simple
mechanics that are fun and are retaining.

1217
00:55:57,320 --> 00:56:00,840
So definitely, encourage
you to have fun with that.

1218
00:56:01,591 --> 00:56:04,978
Having little depth required,
and gaining that depth over time.

1219
00:56:04,978 --> 00:56:07,731
So, thank you so much
for joining this call.

1220
00:56:07,731 --> 00:56:09,749
I've had an absolute blast.

1221
00:56:09,749 --> 00:56:14,337
I hope there's been a few nuggets in here
for you to take back on your builds.
